WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:00.116 --> 00:01.856 align:middle
Transcript of Chair Powell's
Press Conference January 26,

00:01.856 --> 00:02.706 align:middle
2022 CHAIR POWELL.

00:02.706 --> 00:03.016 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:03.016 --> 00:05.056 align:middle
At the Federal Reserve,
we are strongly committed

00:05.056 --> 00:07.106 align:middle
to achieving the
monetary policy goals

00:07.106 --> 00:08.336 align:middle
that Congress has given us:

00:08.846 --> 00:10.846 align:middle
maximum employment
and price stability.

00:12.066 --> 00:13.776 align:middle
Today, in support
of these goals,

00:13.816 --> 00:16.146 align:middle
the Federal Open Market
Committee kept its policy

00:16.146 --> 00:19.666 align:middle
interest rate near zero
and stated its expectation

00:19.766 --> 00:22.076 align:middle
that an increase in this rate
would soon be appropriate.

00:22.596 --> 00:24.596 align:middle
The Committee also agreed

00:24.596 --> 00:26.846 align:middle
to continue reducing
its net asset purchases

00:26.906 --> 00:30.136 align:middle
on the schedule we announced
in December-bringing them

00:30.136 --> 00:31.566 align:middle
to an end in early March.

00:32.596 --> 00:36.156 align:middle
As I will explain, against a
backdrop of elevated inflation

00:36.156 --> 00:39.306 align:middle
and a strong labor market,
our policy has been adapting

00:39.306 --> 00:41.216 align:middle
to the evolving economic
environment,

00:41.846 --> 00:43.186 align:middle
and it will continue to do so.

00:45.186 --> 00:48.476 align:middle
Economic activity expanded
at a robust pace last year,

00:49.186 --> 00:52.396 align:middle
reflecting progress on
vaccinations and the reopening

00:52.396 --> 00:56.066 align:middle
of the economy, fiscal and
monetary policy support,

00:56.346 --> 00:57.866 align:middle
and the healthy financial
positions

00:57.866 --> 00:59.136 align:middle
of households and businesses.

00:59.696 --> 01:02.906 align:middle
Indeed, the economy has shown
great strength and resilience

01:02.936 --> 01:04.926 align:middle
in the face of the
ongoing pandemic.

01:06.286 --> 01:08.836 align:middle
The recent sharp rise in
COVID cases associated

01:08.836 --> 01:11.456 align:middle
with the Omicron
variant will surely weigh

01:11.456 --> 01:13.006 align:middle
on economic growth this quarter.

01:14.036 --> 01:16.866 align:middle
High-frequency indicators
point to reduced spending

01:16.866 --> 01:20.486 align:middle
in COVID-sensitive sectors,
such as travel and restaurants.

01:21.636 --> 01:24.046 align:middle
And activity more broadly
may also be affected

01:24.046 --> 01:26.536 align:middle
as many workers are
unable to report for work

01:26.646 --> 01:29.896 align:middle
because of illness, quarantines,
or caregiving needs.

01:31.136 --> 01:33.736 align:middle
Fortunately, health
experts are finding

01:33.736 --> 01:36.206 align:middle
that the Omicron variant
has not been as virulent

01:36.646 --> 01:39.786 align:middle
as previous strains of
the virus, and they expect

01:39.786 --> 01:41.496 align:middle
that cases will drop
off rapidly.

01:42.676 --> 01:46.426 align:middle
If the wave passes quickly, the
economic effects should as well,

01:46.876 --> 01:48.716 align:middle
and we would see a
return to strong growth.

01:50.206 --> 01:52.956 align:middle
That said, the implications for
the economy remain uncertain.

01:53.876 --> 01:55.556 align:middle
And we have not lost
sight of the fact

01:55.556 --> 01:57.976 align:middle
that for many afflicted
individuals and families,

01:58.546 --> 02:00.646 align:middle
and for the health-care
workers on the front lines,

02:01.196 --> 02:03.586 align:middle
the virus continues to
cause great hardship.

02:05.316 --> 02:08.586 align:middle
The labor market has made
remarkable progress and,

02:08.586 --> 02:10.306 align:middle
by many measures,
is very strong.

02:11.026 --> 02:13.216 align:middle
Job gains have been
solid in recent months,

02:13.536 --> 02:17.266 align:middle
averaging 365,000 per month
over the past three months.

02:18.326 --> 02:20.696 align:middle
Over the past year,
payroll employment has risen

02:20.696 --> 02:22.776 align:middle
by 6.4 million jobs.

02:23.616 --> 02:26.026 align:middle
The unemployment rate
has declined sharply,

02:26.026 --> 02:29.146 align:middle
falling 2 percentage points
over the past six months

02:29.756 --> 02:32.146 align:middle
to reach 3.9 percent
in December.

02:32.936 --> 02:36.756 align:middle
The improvements in labor market
conditions have been widespread,

02:36.956 --> 02:38.686 align:middle
including for workers
at the lower end

02:38.686 --> 02:39.866 align:middle
of the wage distribution,

02:40.226 --> 02:42.486 align:middle
as well as for African
Americans and Hispanics.

02:43.646 --> 02:46.286 align:middle
Labor demand remains
historically strong.

02:47.566 --> 02:49.106 align:middle
With constraints
on labor supply,

02:49.106 --> 02:51.286 align:middle
employers are having
difficulties filling job

02:51.286 --> 02:53.246 align:middle
openings, and wages are rising

02:53.246 --> 02:55.456 align:middle
at their fastest
pace in many years.

02:56.776 --> 02:58.916 align:middle
While labor force
participation has edged up,

02:59.476 --> 03:02.426 align:middle
it remains subdued, in
part reflecting the aging

03:02.426 --> 03:04.266 align:middle
of the population
and retirements.

03:05.396 --> 03:08.856 align:middle
In addition, some who would
otherwise would be seeking work

03:08.946 --> 03:10.916 align:middle
report that they are
out of the labor force

03:10.916 --> 03:12.866 align:middle
because of factors
related to the pandemic,

03:13.316 --> 03:14.766 align:middle
including caregiving needs

03:14.766 --> 03:16.836 align:middle
and ongoing concerns
about the virus.

03:18.286 --> 03:21.276 align:middle
The current wave of the virus
may well prolong these effects.

03:22.216 --> 03:23.556 align:middle
Over time, there
are good reasons

03:23.556 --> 03:25.646 align:middle
to expect some further
improvements

03:25.646 --> 03:27.356 align:middle
in participation and employment.

03:29.096 --> 03:33.056 align:middle
Inflation remains well above our
longer-run goal of 2 percent.

03:33.886 --> 03:36.896 align:middle
Supply and demand imbalances
related to the pandemic

03:36.896 --> 03:39.036 align:middle
and [to] the reopening of
the economy have continued

03:39.036 --> 03:41.736 align:middle
to contribute to elevated
levels of inflation.

03:42.716 --> 03:43.956 align:middle
In particular, bottlenecks

03:43.956 --> 03:46.736 align:middle
and supply constraints are
limiting how quickly production

03:46.796 --> 03:49.226 align:middle
can respond to higher
demand in the near term.

03:50.456 --> 03:53.156 align:middle
These problems have been
larger and longer lasting

03:53.156 --> 03:56.596 align:middle
than anticipated, exacerbated
by waves of the virus.

03:57.966 --> 03:59.976 align:middle
While the drivers of
higher inflation have been

03:59.976 --> 04:02.496 align:middle
predominantly connected
to the dislocations caused

04:02.496 --> 04:05.336 align:middle
by the pandemic, price
increases have now spread

04:05.366 --> 04:07.776 align:middle
to a broader range of
goods and services.

04:08.526 --> 04:11.666 align:middle
Wages have also risen
briskly, and we are attentive

04:11.666 --> 04:14.616 align:middle
to the risks that persistent
real wage growth in excess

04:14.616 --> 04:17.246 align:middle
of productivity [growth]
could put upward pressure

04:17.246 --> 04:17.946 align:middle
on inflation.

04:19.416 --> 04:22.066 align:middle
Like most forecasters, we
continue to expect inflation

04:22.066 --> 04:23.956 align:middle
to decline over the
course of the year.

04:25.886 --> 04:28.946 align:middle
We understand that high
inflation imposes significant

04:28.946 --> 04:31.386 align:middle
hardship, especially
on those least able

04:31.386 --> 04:34.276 align:middle
to meet the higher costs
of essentials like food,

04:34.436 --> 04:35.756 align:middle
housing, and transportation.

04:36.586 --> 04:38.946 align:middle
In addition, we believe that
the best thing we can do

04:38.946 --> 04:41.436 align:middle
to support continued
labor market gains is

04:41.436 --> 04:43.066 align:middle
to promote a long expansion,

04:43.836 --> 04:45.596 align:middle
and that will require
price stability.

04:45.706 --> 04:48.746 align:middle
We are committed to our
price-stability goal.

04:49.386 --> 04:51.886 align:middle
We will use our tools both
to support the economy

04:51.886 --> 04:54.936 align:middle
and a strong labor market and
to prevent higher inflation

04:54.936 --> 04:56.206 align:middle
from becoming entrenched.

04:56.716 --> 04:59.126 align:middle
And we'll be watching carefully

04:59.126 --> 05:00.906 align:middle
to see whether the
economy is evolving

05:00.906 --> 05:02.326 align:middle
in line with expectations.

05:04.336 --> 05:06.996 align:middle
The Fed's monetary policy
actions have been guided

05:06.996 --> 05:09.586 align:middle
by our mandate to
promote maximum employment

05:09.586 --> 05:11.526 align:middle
and stable prices for
the American people.

05:12.596 --> 05:15.066 align:middle
As I noted, the Committee
left the target range

05:15.066 --> 05:16.826 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate unchanged

05:17.186 --> 05:19.546 align:middle
and reaffirmed our plan,
announced in December,

05:20.016 --> 05:22.246 align:middle
to end asset purchases
in early March.

05:23.476 --> 05:25.316 align:middle
In light of the remarkable
progress we've seen

05:25.316 --> 05:27.726 align:middle
in the labor market and
inflation that is well

05:27.726 --> 05:29.366 align:middle
above our 2 percent
longer-run goal,

05:30.036 --> 05:32.726 align:middle
the economy no longer
needs sustained high levels

05:32.726 --> 05:34.136 align:middle
of monetary policy support.

05:35.026 --> 05:37.286 align:middle
That is why we are phasing
out our asset purchases

05:37.446 --> 05:40.426 align:middle
and why we expect it
will soon be appropriate

05:40.426 --> 05:42.526 align:middle
to raise the target range
for the federal funds rate.

05:43.116 --> 05:46.826 align:middle
Of course, the economic outlook
remains highly uncertain.

05:47.726 --> 05:49.656 align:middle
Making appropriate
monetary policy

05:49.656 --> 05:51.566 align:middle
in this environment
requires humility,

05:52.046 --> 05:55.316 align:middle
recognizing that the economy
evolves in unexpected ways.

05:55.926 --> 05:59.136 align:middle
We'll need to be nimble so that
we can respond to the full range

05:59.136 --> 06:00.316 align:middle
of plausible outcomes.

06:01.186 --> 06:04.616 align:middle
With this in mind, we will
remain attentive to risks,

06:04.876 --> 06:07.596 align:middle
including the risk that high
inflation is more persistent

06:07.596 --> 06:10.496 align:middle
than expected, and are
prepared to respond

06:10.496 --> 06:12.506 align:middle
as appropriate to
achieve our goals.

06:13.056 --> 06:16.766 align:middle
To provide greater
clarity about our approach

06:16.766 --> 06:19.266 align:middle
for reducing the size of the
Federal Reserve's balance sheet,

06:19.416 --> 06:21.376 align:middle
today the Committee
issued a set of principles

06:21.376 --> 06:23.996 align:middle
that will provide a foundation
for our future decisions.

06:24.846 --> 06:26.496 align:middle
[In] these high-level
principles, [we] clarify

06:26.856 --> 06:29.216 align:middle
that the federal funds
rate is our primary means

06:29.216 --> 06:30.686 align:middle
of adjusting monetary policy

06:31.436 --> 06:33.446 align:middle
and that reducing our
balance sheet will occur

06:33.546 --> 06:36.446 align:middle
after the process of raising
interest rates has begun.

06:37.866 --> 06:39.586 align:middle
Reductions will occur over time

06:39.586 --> 06:42.306 align:middle
in a predictable manner
primarily through adjustments

06:42.306 --> 06:45.266 align:middle
to reinvestments so that
securities roll off our

06:45.266 --> 06:45.906 align:middle
balance sheet.

06:47.046 --> 06:50.166 align:middle
Over time, we intend to hold
securities in the amounts needed

06:50.166 --> 06:52.826 align:middle
for our ample-reserves
operating framework,

06:53.606 --> 06:54.596 align:middle
and in the longer run,

06:54.596 --> 06:56.906 align:middle
we envision holding
primarily Treasury securities.

06:57.796 --> 07:00.346 align:middle
Our decisions to reduce our
balance sheet will be guided

07:00.346 --> 07:03.256 align:middle
by our maximum-employment
and price-stability goals.

07:04.476 --> 07:06.976 align:middle
In that regard, we will
be prepared to adjust any

07:06.976 --> 07:09.436 align:middle
of the details of our approach
to balance sheet management

07:09.906 --> 07:12.136 align:middle
in light of economic and
financial developments.

07:12.286 --> 07:16.326 align:middle
The Committee has not made
decisions regarding the specific

07:16.326 --> 07:19.306 align:middle
timing, pace, or other details
of shrinking the balance sheet.

07:19.876 --> 07:22.606 align:middle
And we will discuss these
matters in upcoming meetings

07:23.386 --> 07:24.866 align:middle
and provide additional
information

07:24.866 --> 07:26.446 align:middle
at the appropriate time.

07:27.156 --> 07:30.436 align:middle
To conclude: We understand that
our actions affect communities,

07:30.756 --> 07:32.666 align:middle
families, and businesses
across the country.

07:33.576 --> 07:35.946 align:middle
Everything we do is in
service to our public mission.

07:36.826 --> 07:38.916 align:middle
We at the Federal Reserve
will do everything we can

07:38.916 --> 07:40.556 align:middle
to achieve our
maximum-employment

07:40.556 --> 07:41.786 align:middle
and price-stability goals.

07:42.456 --> 07:42.946 align:middle
Thank you.

07:42.946 --> 07:44.226 align:middle
I look forward to
your questions.

07:44.226 --> 07:44.786 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:46.336 --> 07:46.586 align:middle
Thank you.

07:46.646 --> 07:50.966 align:middle
For the first question,
we'll go to Chris Rugaber

07:50.966 --> 07:53.446 align:middle
at the Associated Press.

07:53.606 --> 07:54.386 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER RUGABER.

07:54.386 --> 07:55.056 align:middle
Thanks, Michelle.

07:55.056 --> 07:56.426 align:middle
And thank you, Chair Powell.

07:56.936 --> 08:04.076 align:middle
So it's expected that the Fed
will hike rates perhaps every

08:04.076 --> 08:08.316 align:middle
other meeting, but
certainly in the past,

08:08.316 --> 08:09.966 align:middle
the Fed has hiked
at every meeting.

08:09.966 --> 08:11.966 align:middle
So I just wanted to ask,
you know, are rate hikes

08:11.966 --> 08:14.886 align:middle
at consecutive meetings
on the table this year?

08:14.886 --> 08:17.326 align:middle
Is every meeting a
live-a live meeting?

08:17.326 --> 08:22.316 align:middle
And, on that note, would the
Fed consider front-loading some

08:22.316 --> 08:25.906 align:middle
of its rate hikes, even if it
doesn't raise at every meeting?

08:25.906 --> 08:26.806 align:middle
Thank you.

08:26.806 --> 08:27.246 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

08:27.246 --> 08:30.206 align:middle
Thanks. So, as I-as I referred
to in my opening statement,

08:30.636 --> 08:32.596 align:middle
it's-it is not possible
to predict

08:32.596 --> 08:35.116 align:middle
with much confidence
exactly what path

08:35.116 --> 08:37.466 align:middle
for our policy rate is
going to prove appropriate.

08:38.376 --> 08:41.006 align:middle
And so, at this time, we
haven't made any decisions

08:41.006 --> 08:42.176 align:middle
about the path of policy.

08:42.326 --> 08:45.206 align:middle
And I stress again that
we'll be humble and nimble.

08:45.206 --> 08:47.606 align:middle
We're going to have to
navigate crosscurrents

08:47.606 --> 08:50.706 align:middle
and actually two-sided
risks now.

08:51.436 --> 08:54.876 align:middle
So-and, and I'll say
also that we're going

08:54.876 --> 08:56.086 align:middle
to be guided by the data.

08:56.086 --> 08:58.236 align:middle
In fact, what I'll say is
that we're going to be led

08:58.236 --> 09:00.876 align:middle
by the incoming data and
the evolving outlook,

09:01.336 --> 09:05.796 align:middle
which we'll try to communicate
as clearly as possible,

09:05.796 --> 09:09.116 align:middle
moving steadily and
transparency-transparently.

09:09.406 --> 09:13.986 align:middle
So more to your question,
we know that the economy is

09:13.986 --> 09:15.556 align:middle
in a very different
place than it was

09:15.556 --> 09:18.346 align:middle
when we began raising
rates in 2015.

09:18.896 --> 09:21.266 align:middle
Specifically, the economy
is now much stronger.

09:22.066 --> 09:24.016 align:middle
The labor market
is far stronger.

09:24.016 --> 09:26.236 align:middle
Inflation is running well
above our 2 percent target,

09:26.316 --> 09:27.716 align:middle
much higher than it
was at that time.

09:28.406 --> 09:31.806 align:middle
And these differences are likely
to have important implications

09:31.806 --> 09:33.836 align:middle
for the appropriate pace
of policy adjustments.

09:34.416 --> 09:36.176 align:middle
Beyond that, we haven't
made any decisions.

09:36.176 --> 09:36.906 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:40.286 --> 09:40.646 align:middle
Thank you.

09:40.646 --> 09:42.346 align:middle
Let's go to Victoria
Guida at Politico.

09:42.346 --> 09:45.806 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

09:45.806 --> 09:46.326 align:middle
Hi, Chair Powell.

09:46.326 --> 09:48.046 align:middle
I wanted to ask,
you were talking

09:48.046 --> 09:49.556 align:middle
about the health of
the labor market.

09:49.556 --> 09:51.916 align:middle
And I'm curious whether you
would characterize where we're

09:51.916 --> 09:54.296 align:middle
at right now as maximum
employment.

09:54.296 --> 09:56.546 align:middle
And also, along those
same lines,

09:56.996 --> 09:59.876 align:middle
obviously rate hikes [are]
on the table this year,

09:59.876 --> 10:03.606 align:middle
do you think that the Fed can
raise rates, bring inflation

10:03.606 --> 10:05.916 align:middle
under control without
hurting jobs and wages?

10:10.516 --> 10:10.746 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

10:10.846 --> 10:11.736 align:middle
Sorry. Just getting both,

10:11.736 --> 10:14.176 align:middle
both parts of your
question written down.

10:14.536 --> 10:18.816 align:middle
So I would say-and this
view is widely held

10:18.816 --> 10:21.316 align:middle
on the Committee-that both
sides of the mandate are calling

10:21.316 --> 10:23.046 align:middle
for us to move steadily away

10:23.376 --> 10:25.766 align:middle
from the very highly
accommodative policies we put

10:25.766 --> 10:28.806 align:middle
in place during the
challenging economic conditions

10:29.206 --> 10:31.146 align:middle
that the economy faced
earlier in the pandemic.

10:32.126 --> 10:34.636 align:middle
And I would say that most
FOMC participants agree

10:34.636 --> 10:36.806 align:middle
that labor market
conditions are consistent

10:36.806 --> 10:39.936 align:middle
with maximum employment in
the sense of the highest level

10:39.936 --> 10:42.096 align:middle
of employment that is
consistent with price stability.

10:42.176 --> 10:43.796 align:middle
And that is-that is
my personal view.

10:44.626 --> 10:47.556 align:middle
And, and, again, very broad
support on the Committee

10:47.556 --> 10:49.546 align:middle
for the judgment that it
will soon be appropriate

10:49.546 --> 10:52.576 align:middle
to raise the target range
for the federal funds rate.

10:53.326 --> 10:57.176 align:middle
The other thing is,
maximum employment will,

10:57.216 --> 10:59.686 align:middle
will evolve over time
and through the course

10:59.686 --> 11:00.516 align:middle
of the business cycle.

11:00.516 --> 11:01.946 align:middle
In the particular
situation we're

11:01.946 --> 11:04.366 align:middle
in now, it may well increase.

11:04.616 --> 11:07.446 align:middle
Max-the level of maximum-of
employment that's consistent

11:07.446 --> 11:08.996 align:middle
with stable prices may increase.

11:09.496 --> 11:12.186 align:middle
And we hope that it will,
as more people come back

11:12.186 --> 11:15.096 align:middle
into the labor market, as
participation gradually rises.

11:15.666 --> 11:19.936 align:middle
And the policy path that we're
broadly contemplating would be

11:19.936 --> 11:22.026 align:middle
supportive of that
comment-that outcome as well.

11:23.176 --> 11:26.356 align:middle
So the thing about the
labor market right now is

11:26.356 --> 11:28.426 align:middle
that there are-there
are many millions of,

11:28.426 --> 11:31.406 align:middle
of more job openings than
there are unemployed people.

11:31.876 --> 11:35.486 align:middle
So you ask whether we
can-whether we can raise rates

11:35.486 --> 11:38.076 align:middle
and, and move to less
accommodative, and even tight,

11:38.076 --> 11:39.826 align:middle
financial conditions without
hurting the labor market.

11:40.026 --> 11:43.636 align:middle
I think there's quite a bit of
room to raise interest rates

11:44.126 --> 11:45.406 align:middle
without threatening
the labor market.

11:45.406 --> 11:50.256 align:middle
This is, by so many measures, a
historically tight labor market.

11:50.756 --> 11:54.396 align:middle
Record levels of job
openings, of quits.

11:54.886 --> 11:57.876 align:middle
Wages are moving up at
the highest pace they have

11:57.876 --> 11:58.856 align:middle
in decades.

11:59.336 --> 12:02.876 align:middle
If you look at surveys
of workers,

12:02.876 --> 12:04.196 align:middle
they find jobs plentiful.

12:04.196 --> 12:04.866 align:middle
Look at surveys

12:04.866 --> 12:06.826 align:middle
of companies-they
find workers scarce.

12:07.296 --> 12:09.326 align:middle
And all of those readings
are at levels, really,

12:09.326 --> 12:12.876 align:middle
that we haven't seen in a long
time-and, in some cases, ever.

12:12.876 --> 12:15.546 align:middle
So this is a very, very
strong labor market,

12:15.996 --> 12:20.756 align:middle
and my strong sense is that we
can-we can-we can move rates

12:20.756 --> 12:26.136 align:middle
up without, without having to,
you know, severely undermine it.

12:26.646 --> 12:29.246 align:middle
I also would point out that
there are-there are other forces

12:29.246 --> 12:32.046 align:middle
at work this year, which should
also help bring down inflation,

12:32.396 --> 12:34.856 align:middle
we hope, including
improvement on the supply side,

12:34.856 --> 12:36.176 align:middle
which will ultimately come.

12:36.686 --> 12:38.376 align:middle
The timing and pace
of that are uncertain.

12:38.376 --> 12:42.506 align:middle
And also, fiscal policy is
going to be less supportive of,

12:42.626 --> 12:45.356 align:middle
of growth this year-not of the
level of economic activity,

12:45.356 --> 12:48.686 align:middle
but the fiscal impulse to growth
will be significantly lower.

12:48.686 --> 12:50.986 align:middle
So there are multiple
forces-which should be working

12:51.526 --> 12:55.046 align:middle
over the course of the year
for inflation to come down.

12:55.046 --> 12:57.856 align:middle
We do realize that the
timing and pace of that are,

12:58.246 --> 13:02.016 align:middle
are highly uncertain and that
inflation has persisted longer

13:02.016 --> 13:03.506 align:middle
than we-than we thought.

13:04.256 --> 13:07.166 align:middle
And, of course, we're prepared
to use our tools to assure

13:07.716 --> 13:10.396 align:middle
that higher inflation does
not become entrenched.

13:10.686 --> 13:10.966 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

13:13.886 --> 13:14.236 align:middle
Thank you.

13:14.236 --> 13:16.206 align:middle
Let's go to Nick Timiraos
at the Wall Street Journal.

13:16.206 --> 13:18.316 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

13:18.316 --> 13:19.266 align:middle
Good afternoon, Chair Powell.

13:19.266 --> 13:20.966 align:middle
Nick Timiraos of the
Wall Street Journal.

13:21.736 --> 13:24.126 align:middle
I, I have a couple of
questions on the balance sheet.

13:24.126 --> 13:26.856 align:middle
The statement on the
balance sheet today calls for

13:26.856 --> 13:29.206 align:middle
"significantly reducing"
your holdings.

13:29.916 --> 13:30.966 align:middle
What does that mean?

13:30.966 --> 13:34.076 align:middle
And then, apart from
moving sooner and faster

13:34.076 --> 13:36.596 align:middle
to shrink the holdings,
are there any other ways

13:36.936 --> 13:39.766 align:middle
in which you and your colleagues
are seriously thinking

13:39.766 --> 13:43.066 align:middle
about recalibrating
this process?

13:43.516 --> 13:46.246 align:middle
And, finally, how much
disagreement is there

13:46.506 --> 13:48.766 align:middle
around how you should
use this tool,

13:49.066 --> 13:53.116 align:middle
including active sales rather
than passive sales or changes

13:53.116 --> 13:55.486 align:middle
in the composition
of reinvestments?

13:55.486 --> 13:55.916 align:middle
Thank you.

13:55.916 --> 13:56.126 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

13:56.126 --> 14:01.166 align:middle
So I'm afraid to tell you that
those are all great questions,

14:01.166 --> 14:03.316 align:middle
and they're questions that
the Committee is just turning

14:03.316 --> 14:03.786 align:middle
to now.

14:03.786 --> 14:06.166 align:middle
So we had-we had a
discussion, as you know,

14:06.166 --> 14:08.746 align:middle
at the last meeting, an
introductory discussion

14:08.746 --> 14:10.826 align:middle
of the balance sheet and
a teeing up of the issues.

14:10.826 --> 14:14.996 align:middle
At this meeting,
we've gone through

14:14.996 --> 14:17.966 align:middle
and carefully put together a set
of principles at a high level.

14:18.376 --> 14:20.616 align:middle
And those are meant to guide
the actual decisions we'll make

14:20.616 --> 14:22.676 align:middle
about the pace and about
all of the questions

14:22.676 --> 14:24.056 align:middle
that you're, you're asking.

14:24.056 --> 14:28.216 align:middle
And I expect that this
process will, will be something

14:28.216 --> 14:30.526 align:middle
that we spend time on
in coming meetings.

14:30.796 --> 14:31.886 align:middle
I can't tell you how many.

14:31.886 --> 14:33.096 align:middle
I can't tell you how
long it will take.

14:33.096 --> 14:36.476 align:middle
But-and then, you know,
at the appropriate time,

14:36.476 --> 14:38.146 align:middle
we'll provide additional
information.

14:38.616 --> 14:41.316 align:middle
So I did-the last cycle
when we-when we went

14:41.316 --> 14:44.576 align:middle
through balance sheet
issues, we did find that over,

14:44.576 --> 14:46.846 align:middle
over the course of two or
three meetings, for example,

14:47.166 --> 14:49.856 align:middle
we did come to interesting and
better answers, we thought.

14:49.856 --> 14:52.626 align:middle
So we're just in
that process now.

14:52.626 --> 14:55.426 align:middle
And at the next meeting,
we'll be turning to, you know,

14:55.426 --> 14:59.376 align:middle
more of the details that
you're-that you're asking about.

14:59.376 --> 14:59.996 align:middle
I would say this.

14:59.996 --> 15:02.236 align:middle
The balance sheet
is, is much bigger.

15:02.806 --> 15:05.956 align:middle
I'd say it has a shorter
duration than the last time.

15:06.636 --> 15:09.306 align:middle
And the economy's much stronger,
and inflation is much higher.

15:09.836 --> 15:13.246 align:middle
So-and I think that
leads you to-and I said,

15:13.246 --> 15:16.136 align:middle
I've said this-being
willing to move sooner

15:16.136 --> 15:19.956 align:middle
than we did the last time
and also perhaps faster.

15:20.136 --> 15:21.336 align:middle
But beyond that,

15:21.476 --> 15:23.676 align:middle
it's really-it's really
not appropriate for me

15:23.676 --> 15:26.186 align:middle
to speculate exactly
what that would be.

15:26.186 --> 15:28.576 align:middle
And, and-but I would point
you to principle number one,

15:28.576 --> 15:31.276 align:middle
which is "the Committee views
changes in the target range

15:31.276 --> 15:33.966 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate as its primary means

15:33.966 --> 15:36.036 align:middle
of adjusting the stance
of monetary policy."

15:36.556 --> 15:39.316 align:middle
So we do want the
federal funds rate-we,

15:39.346 --> 15:41.746 align:middle
we want to operationalize
that [as the primary means].

15:42.036 --> 15:45.166 align:middle
And we want the balance
sheet to be declining

15:45.166 --> 15:47.206 align:middle
in a predictable
manner, and we want it

15:47.206 --> 15:50.356 align:middle
to be declining primarily
by adjusting reinvestments.

15:50.356 --> 15:50.956 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

15:52.506 --> 15:55.186 align:middle
So, if I could follow
on that, raising rates

15:55.186 --> 15:58.146 align:middle
and reducing the balance sheet
both restrain the economy,

15:58.426 --> 16:00.096 align:middle
both tighten monetary policy.

16:00.476 --> 16:03.316 align:middle
How should we think about the
relationship between the two?

16:03.556 --> 16:06.696 align:middle
For example, how much
passive runoff is equal

16:06.696 --> 16:11.056 align:middle
to every ¼ percentage point
increase in your benchmark rate?

16:11.056 --> 16:11.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

16:11.446 --> 16:15.256 align:middle
So, again, we think of
the balance sheet as,

16:15.346 --> 16:17.896 align:middle
as moving in a predictable
manner,

16:18.236 --> 16:21.346 align:middle
sort of in the background, and
that the active tool meeting

16:21.346 --> 16:24.066 align:middle
to meeting is not both of them,
it's the federal funds rate.

16:24.706 --> 16:27.746 align:middle
There are-there are rules of
thumb-I'm reluctant to land

16:27.746 --> 16:29.416 align:middle
on one of them-that,
that equate this.

16:29.416 --> 16:32.226 align:middle
And-but there's also an
element of, of uncertainty

16:32.226 --> 16:32.966 align:middle
around the balance sheet.

16:32.966 --> 16:34.826 align:middle
I think we have a much
better sense, frankly,

16:34.826 --> 16:39.066 align:middle
of how rate increases affect
financial conditions and,

16:39.066 --> 16:40.296 align:middle
hence, economic conditions.

16:40.616 --> 16:42.136 align:middle
Balance sheet [policy] is,

16:42.136 --> 16:46.936 align:middle
is still a relatively new thing
for, for the markets and for us,

16:46.936 --> 16:48.456 align:middle
so we're less certain
about that.

16:48.456 --> 16:52.136 align:middle
So, again, our-I think our-the
pattern we'll follow is to-is

16:52.136 --> 16:54.806 align:middle
to arrive at a, you
know, a timing and a pace

16:55.096 --> 16:58.126 align:middle
and composition and all those
things and then announce

16:58.126 --> 17:00.076 align:middle
that with advance notice, and,

17:00.076 --> 17:03.146 align:middle
and it will-it will
start in the background.

17:03.146 --> 17:05.746 align:middle
And then we'll look to have that
just running in the background

17:05.746 --> 17:08.636 align:middle
and have-and have the
interest rates, again,

17:08.806 --> 17:11.256 align:middle
be the active tool
of monetary policy.

17:11.376 --> 17:12.866 align:middle
That's, that's at
least the plan.

17:13.146 --> 17:15.986 align:middle
I can't tell you much
more about any of the-any

17:15.986 --> 17:19.866 align:middle
of the very good issues about
size, pace, composition,

17:19.866 --> 17:20.526 align:middle
those sorts of things.

17:20.526 --> 17:22.666 align:middle
But we'll be turning to all
of those at coming meetings.

17:22.666 --> 17:23.306 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

17:24.616 --> 17:24.896 align:middle
Thank you.

17:24.896 --> 17:25.616 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

17:26.766 --> 17:28.196 align:middle
Thanks. Now we'll
go to Neil Irwin.

17:28.196 --> 17:29.746 align:middle
NEIL IRWIN.

17:30.466 --> 17:31.476 align:middle
Thank you, Chair Powell.

17:31.476 --> 17:32.706 align:middle
It's Neil Irwin from Axios.

17:32.706 --> 17:34.246 align:middle
Glad to be back.

17:34.246 --> 17:36.916 align:middle
Sir, I was wondering if
the volatility we've seen

17:36.916 --> 17:39.296 align:middle
in financial markets in the
last few weeks strikes you

17:39.296 --> 17:42.036 align:middle
as anything alarming or that
might affect the trajectory

17:42.036 --> 17:42.736 align:middle
of policy.

17:43.026 --> 17:44.446 align:middle
Conversely, to the degree

17:44.446 --> 17:46.596 align:middle
that financial conditions
have tightened some,

17:46.596 --> 17:48.816 align:middle
might that be desirable in
some ways in achieving your,

17:49.096 --> 17:50.156 align:middle
your tightening goals?

17:51.016 --> 17:51.376 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

17:51.646 --> 17:55.146 align:middle
So, as you know, the
ultimate focus that we have is

17:55.146 --> 17:57.906 align:middle
on the real economy: maximum
employment and price stability.

17:57.906 --> 18:00.276 align:middle
And financial conditions
matter to the extent

18:00.276 --> 18:02.466 align:middle
that they have implications
for achieving the dual mandate.

18:02.816 --> 18:04.156 align:middle
And you also know that we,

18:04.156 --> 18:06.196 align:middle
we look at broader
financial conditions, not one

18:06.196 --> 18:08.106 align:middle
or two things, one
or two markets.

18:08.266 --> 18:10.046 align:middle
And what we're always
asking ourselves is,

18:10.406 --> 18:13.426 align:middle
are we seeing changes
that are both persistent

18:13.426 --> 18:15.936 align:middle
and material enough
that-of a change

18:15.936 --> 18:19.066 align:middle
in financial conditions-that
they are inconsistent

18:19.066 --> 18:20.106 align:middle
with the achievement
of our goals?

18:20.106 --> 18:21.736 align:middle
So that's how we're
looking at that.

18:21.736 --> 18:24.176 align:middle
And I don't want to comment

18:24.176 --> 18:27.986 align:middle
on today's financial conditions
broadly, but we're not looking

18:27.986 --> 18:29.556 align:middle
at any one market or, or so.

18:29.556 --> 18:31.276 align:middle
So that's how we're thinking.

18:31.736 --> 18:35.756 align:middle
In terms of what we've
seen, I would say this.

18:36.066 --> 18:37.256 align:middle
We, you know, we said

18:37.256 --> 18:40.496 align:middle
at our last meeting we
published the Summary

18:40.496 --> 18:41.536 align:middle
of Economic Projections,

18:41.536 --> 18:45.326 align:middle
the median of which-the median
participant expected three rate

18:45.326 --> 18:46.296 align:middle
increases this year.

18:46.976 --> 18:50.546 align:middle
And, you know, it's six
weeks, seven weeks later now.

18:50.986 --> 18:54.816 align:middle
And you have seen that
our communication channel

18:54.816 --> 18:55.896 align:middle
with the markets is working.

18:55.896 --> 19:00.316 align:middle
Markets are, are now pricing
in a number of rate increases.

19:00.316 --> 19:05.316 align:middle
Surveys show that market
participants are expecting a

19:05.316 --> 19:07.106 align:middle
balance sheet runoff
to begin, you know,

19:07.606 --> 19:10.006 align:middle
at the appropriate time,

19:10.006 --> 19:11.636 align:middle
sometime later this
year perhaps.

19:11.856 --> 19:13.796 align:middle
We haven't made that
decision yet.

19:13.796 --> 19:17.476 align:middle
So we, we feel like the
communications we have

19:17.516 --> 19:18.676 align:middle
with market participants

19:18.676 --> 19:20.386 align:middle
and with the general
public are working

19:21.096 --> 19:23.586 align:middle
and that financial
conditions are reflecting

19:23.646 --> 19:25.276 align:middle
in advance the decisions
that we make.

19:25.276 --> 19:28.096 align:middle
And monetary policy
works significantly

19:28.096 --> 19:29.286 align:middle
through expectations.

19:29.786 --> 19:33.476 align:middle
So that, that in and of
itself is appropriate.

19:33.476 --> 19:34.126 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

19:37.856 --> 19:38.216 align:middle
Thank you.

19:38.216 --> 19:39.386 align:middle
Let's go to Howard Schneider.

19:39.386 --> 19:42.416 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

19:42.416 --> 19:44.556 align:middle
You know, for a year or so-Hi.

19:44.556 --> 19:45.146 align:middle
Thanks, Chair Powell.

19:45.146 --> 19:46.066 align:middle
Howard Schneider with Reuters.

19:46.266 --> 19:47.636 align:middle
So for a year now,

19:47.636 --> 19:49.946 align:middle
the statements referenced
the benchmarks

19:49.946 --> 19:52.446 align:middle
for this initial
interest rate increase.

19:52.446 --> 19:54.466 align:middle
Now that we're approaching
that moment,

19:54.466 --> 19:55.826 align:middle
what are the benchmarks
going to be

19:55.826 --> 19:57.506 align:middle
for subsequent rate increases?

19:57.506 --> 19:59.926 align:middle
I know you can't stipulate the
path, but how should we think

19:59.926 --> 20:03.506 align:middle
about the criteria for the
next step and the next step?

20:05.106 --> 20:05.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

20:05.816 --> 20:06.316 align:middle
Well, you're right.

20:06.316 --> 20:08.236 align:middle
We haven't got-we haven't
gotten to that point yet.

20:08.236 --> 20:10.066 align:middle
We haven't made a decision
yet, and we'll make

20:10.066 --> 20:11.616 align:middle
that decision at
the March meeting.

20:12.146 --> 20:16.146 align:middle
We'll make a decision whether
to raise the federal funds rate.

20:16.296 --> 20:20.636 align:middle
I would say that the
Committee is, is of a mind to,

20:20.796 --> 20:23.346 align:middle
to raise the federal funds
rate at the March meeting,

20:23.346 --> 20:26.786 align:middle
assuming that conditions are
appropriate for doing so.

20:26.786 --> 20:29.336 align:middle
We have-we have our
eyes on, on the risks,

20:29.336 --> 20:32.186 align:middle
particularly around the world.

20:32.356 --> 20:37.276 align:middle
But we do expect some softening
in the economy from Omicron,

20:37.276 --> 20:38.936 align:middle
but we think that that
should be temporary.

20:38.936 --> 20:42.126 align:middle
And we think that the economy
should-the underlying strength

20:42.126 --> 20:44.586 align:middle
of the economy should,
you know, should,

20:44.946 --> 20:47.506 align:middle
should-show through
fairly quickly after that.

20:47.886 --> 20:50.706 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

20:50.706 --> 20:54.416 align:middle
If I could follow just
on a related question,

20:54.416 --> 20:56.996 align:middle
the December SEPs had
this copacetic sort of set

20:56.996 --> 20:59.936 align:middle
of circumstances
where inflation comes

20:59.936 --> 21:02.936 align:middle
down without the federal
funds rate ever getting

21:02.936 --> 21:05.616 align:middle
over the estimate of neutral.

21:05.616 --> 21:07.016 align:middle
Given developments since then,

21:07.016 --> 21:09.206 align:middle
do you still think that's
a credible narrative

21:09.206 --> 21:10.716 align:middle
for the ultimate path of policy?

21:11.216 --> 21:11.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

21:11.526 --> 21:12.936 align:middle
I think the path
is highly uncertain

21:12.936 --> 21:15.106 align:middle
and that we're committed to
using our tools to make sure

21:15.106 --> 21:17.946 align:middle
that inflation, high
inflation that we're seeing,

21:17.946 --> 21:19.166 align:middle
does not become entrenched.

21:19.606 --> 21:25.096 align:middle
So a number of factors would
be-it's not just monetary

21:25.096 --> 21:28.346 align:middle
policy-a number of factors
are supporting a decline

21:28.346 --> 21:29.936 align:middle
in inflation, as I mentioned.

21:30.216 --> 21:33.586 align:middle
Fiscal policy will be-will
provide significantly less

21:33.586 --> 21:34.526 align:middle
of an impulse to growth.

21:35.136 --> 21:39.126 align:middle
We do expect this
year-although we do expect now

21:39.126 --> 21:41.656 align:middle
that it will come slower
than, than we had expected

21:41.656 --> 21:44.176 align:middle
and hoped-that there will be
relief on the supply side.

21:44.176 --> 21:48.096 align:middle
So that, too, should-should
lower the supply-side barriers,

21:48.096 --> 21:50.236 align:middle
which are a big part of the
story of why inflation is high.

21:50.236 --> 21:53.196 align:middle
In addition, monetary policy
will be becoming significantly

21:53.196 --> 21:54.246 align:middle
less accommodative.

21:54.666 --> 21:56.806 align:middle
So the question is-you
know, we'll,

21:56.806 --> 22:00.386 align:middle
we'll be asking this question
all year long, and that will be,

22:00.546 --> 22:03.696 align:middle
are things turning
out as we expect?

22:03.926 --> 22:06.766 align:middle
There's a case that,
for whatever reason,

22:06.766 --> 22:08.846 align:middle
the economy slows more
and inflation slows more

22:08.846 --> 22:10.336 align:middle
than expected; we'll
react to that.

22:10.736 --> 22:13.586 align:middle
If, instead, we see
inflation at a higher level

22:13.586 --> 22:16.346 align:middle
or a more persistent level,
then we'll react to that.

22:16.686 --> 22:18.726 align:middle
And, again, we're,
we're well aware

22:18.726 --> 22:22.116 align:middle
that this is a different economy
than-than existed during the

22:22.116 --> 22:24.846 align:middle
last tightening cycle,
and our policy is going

22:24.846 --> 22:26.066 align:middle
to reflect those differences.

22:26.066 --> 22:26.856 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

22:27.016 --> 22:27.686 align:middle
Thank you.

22:27.686 --> 22:28.126 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

22:31.446 --> 22:31.806 align:middle
Thank you.

22:31.806 --> 22:33.046 align:middle
Let's go to Jeanna Smialek.

22:42.006 --> 22:42.526 align:middle
Let's go to Jeanna.

22:42.526 --> 22:42.736 align:middle
[No response] Okay.

22:50.616 --> 22:52.396 align:middle
Let's go to Steve Liesman.

22:56.686 --> 22:57.316 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

22:57.316 --> 22:58.646 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle.

22:58.646 --> 22:59.896 align:middle
And thank you, Mr. Chairman.

22:59.896 --> 23:03.016 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, I have one
sort of technical question

23:03.016 --> 23:04.746 align:middle
and one question on principle.

23:04.746 --> 23:07.356 align:middle
The technical question
is, if, if you're going

23:07.356 --> 23:11.236 align:middle
to discuss balance sheet at
the next upcoming meetings

23:11.236 --> 23:13.146 align:middle
and you won't begin
balance sheet reduction

23:13.486 --> 23:16.006 align:middle
until after you begin
rate hikes,

23:16.006 --> 23:18.016 align:middle
it seems to technically
mean that you won't

23:18.016 --> 23:21.326 align:middle
or can't begin balance sheet
reduction until the summer.

23:21.326 --> 23:22.166 align:middle
Is that correct?

23:22.166 --> 23:22.926 align:middle
That's the first thing.

23:23.296 --> 23:25.336 align:middle
Second of all, you
suggested that,

23:25.906 --> 23:27.996 align:middle
that the-with balance
sheet running

23:27.996 --> 23:31.246 align:middle
in the background-that you
would possibly be raising rates

23:31.246 --> 23:33.576 align:middle
and running off the balance
sheet at the same time.

23:33.896 --> 23:36.546 align:middle
That's sort of the
technical question part of it.

23:36.546 --> 23:38.586 align:middle
The principal question I
have is, you said it's going

23:38.586 --> 23:40.506 align:middle
to be running in the background.

23:40.946 --> 23:44.436 align:middle
But the statement on the
balance sheet principles says

23:44.886 --> 23:46.986 align:middle
"the Committee is prepared
to adjust any of the details

23:46.986 --> 23:48.696 align:middle
of its approach"
based on "economic

23:48.696 --> 23:52.266 align:middle
and financial developments,"
which suggests there's something

23:52.266 --> 23:55.736 align:middle
of a reaction function
associated to the balance sheet

23:55.736 --> 23:57.076 align:middle
and it won't be running
in the background.

23:57.076 --> 23:59.946 align:middle
So can you give us any
sense of the discussion

24:00.026 --> 24:03.406 align:middle
or staff presentation on
what is the reaction function

24:03.486 --> 24:04.586 align:middle
surrounding the balance sheet?

24:04.586 --> 24:05.006 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

24:05.006 --> 24:08.116 align:middle
So let me-let me-let
me start by talking

24:08.116 --> 24:09.396 align:middle
about that last paragraph.

24:09.396 --> 24:14.866 align:middle
So you'll remember during the
last cycle that this process

24:14.946 --> 24:18.166 align:middle
of building up and then
shrinking the balance sheet is a

24:18.166 --> 24:21.396 align:middle
complicated one, and it
involves, inevitably, surprises.

24:21.676 --> 24:25.566 align:middle
And so during, during the
years, during the prior cycle,

24:25.566 --> 24:28.376 align:middle
we amended our balance sheet
principles a number of times.

24:28.496 --> 24:29.776 align:middle
Now, we didn't intend
to do that.

24:29.776 --> 24:31.716 align:middle
It just-events required
us to do so.

24:31.716 --> 24:33.776 align:middle
So we got a pretty
robust paragraph there

24:34.156 --> 24:35.976 align:middle
that says we're free
to do this at any time.

24:36.516 --> 24:40.106 align:middle
And it doesn't mean we're going
to, but if the situation turns

24:40.106 --> 24:43.346 align:middle
out to be different than we
had thought, we're not going

24:43.346 --> 24:44.406 align:middle
to be-we're not going to stick

24:44.406 --> 24:45.546 align:middle
with something that
isn't working.

24:45.706 --> 24:46.486 align:middle
That's all that's saying.

24:46.486 --> 24:50.316 align:middle
It's meant to be quite a general
statement rather than a, a hint.

24:50.876 --> 24:54.636 align:middle
So, I mean, I like to think that
our, you know, our philosophy

24:54.636 --> 24:57.216 align:middle
of the balance sheet is, is
embodied in these principles.

24:57.836 --> 25:01.346 align:middle
So, you know, the idea
that, for example,

25:01.346 --> 25:03.826 align:middle
the federal funds rate
is the primary means

25:04.226 --> 25:06.596 align:middle
of adjusting the
stance of policy,

25:07.056 --> 25:09.886 align:middle
that we'll use-determine
the timing and pace

25:09.886 --> 25:11.266 align:middle
of reducing the size
of the balance sheet

25:11.436 --> 25:14.986 align:middle
to foster the dual mandate, that
we'll begin to reduce the size

25:15.106 --> 25:18.606 align:middle
after we begin the
process of raising rates,

25:19.006 --> 25:20.166 align:middle
and on and on like that.

25:20.166 --> 25:21.606 align:middle
I mean, that-those are
all the things that,

25:21.606 --> 25:25.156 align:middle
that try to describe
how we will proceed.

25:25.676 --> 25:29.676 align:middle
But maybe at a higher level,
to try to get at your question:

25:29.676 --> 25:35.176 align:middle
You know, asset purchases
were, were enormously important

25:35.176 --> 25:37.976 align:middle
at the beginning of
the recovery in terms

25:37.976 --> 25:40.306 align:middle
of restoring market function,
as they were at-right

25:40.306 --> 25:43.456 align:middle
after the-in the critical phase
of the Global Financial Crisis.

25:44.006 --> 25:46.726 align:middle
And then after [that], they
were a macroeconomic tool

25:46.726 --> 25:47.716 align:middle
to support demand.

25:48.376 --> 25:51.616 align:middle
And now the economy
no longer needs this,

25:51.966 --> 25:55.556 align:middle
this highly accommodative
policy that we put in place.

25:55.556 --> 25:59.636 align:middle
So it's time to stop asset
purchases first and then,

25:59.726 --> 26:02.566 align:middle
at the appropriate time, start
to shrink the balance sheet.

26:02.706 --> 26:03.696 align:middle
Now, the balance sheet is,

26:04.196 --> 26:06.956 align:middle
is substantially larger
than it needs to be.

26:06.956 --> 26:10.576 align:middle
We've identified the end
state as-in amounts needed

26:10.636 --> 26:13.866 align:middle
to implement monetary policy
efficiently [and] effectively

26:13.866 --> 26:15.096 align:middle
in the ample-reserves regime.

26:15.756 --> 26:20.646 align:middle
So there's a substantial
amount of, of shrinkage

26:20.646 --> 26:21.806 align:middle
in the balance sheet to be done.

26:22.436 --> 26:24.006 align:middle
That's going to take some time.

26:24.006 --> 26:27.556 align:middle
We want that process to be
orderly and predictable.

26:27.556 --> 26:33.916 align:middle
And so those are some of the
ways that I would think this,

26:33.916 --> 26:36.526 align:middle
this lays out our, you know,

26:36.966 --> 26:38.936 align:middle
the way we-the way we're
thinking about this.

26:39.146 --> 26:41.876 align:middle
In terms of the timing,
I can't really help you.

26:41.876 --> 26:44.616 align:middle
You know, it just says after,
after we get under way.

26:45.006 --> 26:47.606 align:middle
So we're-I would say we are,

26:48.426 --> 26:50.256 align:middle
we're going to have
another discussion at,

26:50.256 --> 26:51.176 align:middle
at the next meeting.

26:51.796 --> 26:54.636 align:middle
And my guess is, we'll have
at least one other discussion

26:54.636 --> 26:55.956 align:middle
at the meeting after that.

26:56.436 --> 26:59.986 align:middle
And, you know, we'll, we'll
tell you as we make progress.

27:00.596 --> 27:07.546 align:middle
And we'll, you know, we'll start
the process of allowing runoff

27:07.546 --> 27:09.486 align:middle
and shrinking the balance
sheet at what we find

27:09.486 --> 27:10.556 align:middle
to be the appropriate time.

27:10.676 --> 27:12.246 align:middle
It's-I wish I could say more.

27:12.246 --> 27:14.336 align:middle
But, honestly, we haven't
made those decisions.

27:14.336 --> 27:17.496 align:middle
And we actually haven't
even really had the,

27:17.816 --> 27:20.666 align:middle
the important discussions
on a lot of the details

27:20.666 --> 27:22.136 align:middle
that we will have
at coming meetings.

27:22.316 --> 27:22.746 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

27:25.226 --> 27:25.576 align:middle
Thank you.

27:25.576 --> 27:26.836 align:middle
Let's go to Craig Torres.

27:29.486 --> 27:30.346 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

27:30.346 --> 27:31.316 align:middle
Chair Powell, Chair Powell.

27:31.316 --> 27:33.166 align:middle
Good afternoon, Michelle
and Chair Powell.

27:33.166 --> 27:34.486 align:middle
Craig Torres from Bloomberg.

27:34.486 --> 27:37.746 align:middle
Chair Powell, at the
beginning of the conversation,

27:37.746 --> 27:39.336 align:middle
you said risks are two sided.

27:39.336 --> 27:42.516 align:middle
And I'm wondering if you can
elaborate on what are the risks

27:42.516 --> 27:44.206 align:middle
to the elusive soft landing.

27:44.516 --> 27:46.226 align:middle
Is Fed policy a risk?

27:46.226 --> 27:47.096 align:middle
Overtightening?

27:47.396 --> 27:49.416 align:middle
Or what are the risks?

27:49.416 --> 27:50.556 align:middle
And then second, Chair Powell,

27:50.556 --> 27:53.406 align:middle
I have a quick administrative
question.

27:54.286 --> 27:57.406 align:middle
You know, Robert
Kaplan's disclosure

27:57.406 --> 28:00.186 align:middle
of his securities
transactions-and in a couple

28:00.186 --> 28:01.876 align:middle
of months, Chair
Powell, or maybe sooner,

28:02.306 --> 28:04.396 align:middle
you and I will file
our tax returns.

28:04.396 --> 28:06.846 align:middle
And we'll list transactions
and all kinds of things.

28:06.846 --> 28:10.066 align:middle
And next to those
transactions, we'll put dates.

28:10.066 --> 28:11.956 align:middle
And Bloomberg asked
for the dates

28:11.956 --> 28:13.986 align:middle
of Mr. Kaplan's transactions.

28:14.316 --> 28:17.136 align:middle
The Dallas Fed is not
giving us the dates.

28:17.136 --> 28:19.036 align:middle
And I don't see why
this is a matter

28:19.106 --> 28:23.686 align:middle
for the inspector
general or anybody else.

28:23.686 --> 28:25.666 align:middle
I mean, why can't he
give us the dates?

28:25.666 --> 28:29.096 align:middle
Will you help us get the
dates of those transactions?

28:29.096 --> 28:30.096 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

28:30.096 --> 28:34.326 align:middle
So you asked about
the risks first.

28:34.866 --> 28:39.246 align:middle
So I-you know, the one risk is
that inflation risks are still

28:39.246 --> 28:44.406 align:middle
to the upside in the views of
most FOMC participants and,

28:44.406 --> 28:45.726 align:middle
certainly, in my view as well.

28:46.396 --> 28:49.276 align:middle
There's a risk that, that the
high inflation we're seeing will

28:49.276 --> 28:52.126 align:middle
be prolonged, and there's a risk
that it will move even higher.

28:52.576 --> 28:56.036 align:middle
So we don't-we don't think
that's the base case,

28:56.036 --> 28:57.316 align:middle
but you asked what
the risks are.

28:57.956 --> 29:01.106 align:middle
And that's-we have
to be in a position

29:01.106 --> 29:03.816 align:middle
with our monetary
policy to address all

29:03.816 --> 29:05.086 align:middle
of the plausible outcomes.

29:05.646 --> 29:07.736 align:middle
And that calls for
us to be in position.

29:08.346 --> 29:11.576 align:middle
We, we have an expectation about
the way the economy is going

29:11.576 --> 29:13.566 align:middle
to be evolved, but we've
got to be in a position to,

29:13.666 --> 29:16.616 align:middle
to address different
outcomes, including the one

29:16.616 --> 29:18.016 align:middle
where inflation remains higher.

29:18.846 --> 29:23.376 align:middle
And, of course, that is a risk
to the-to the-to the expansion.

29:23.376 --> 29:25.306 align:middle
You know, we've been saying

29:25.306 --> 29:28.946 align:middle
that what we need here is
another long expansion,

29:29.346 --> 29:32.116 align:middle
which is the kind of thing
we saw over the last [one],

29:32.116 --> 29:33.546 align:middle
which was a record
long expansion.

29:33.866 --> 29:36.456 align:middle
We saw labor force
participation rise.

29:36.456 --> 29:41.106 align:middle
We saw wages persistently higher
for people at the lower end,

29:41.106 --> 29:44.876 align:middle
and there, there really was no
obvious imbalance in the economy

29:44.876 --> 29:46.096 align:middle
that threatened that expansion.

29:46.096 --> 29:49.986 align:middle
It could have gone on for years
were it not hit by the pandemic.

29:49.986 --> 29:52.606 align:middle
So we'd love to find a
way to get back to that.

29:52.606 --> 29:54.846 align:middle
That's going to require price
stability, and that's going

29:54.846 --> 29:58.456 align:middle
to require the Fed to
tighten interest rate policy

29:58.456 --> 30:01.556 align:middle
and do our part in
getting inflation back

30:01.556 --> 30:02.936 align:middle
down to our 2 percent goal.

30:03.646 --> 30:05.546 align:middle
So I mentioned two-sided risks.

30:05.826 --> 30:08.136 align:middle
You know, a couple things.

30:08.136 --> 30:11.526 align:middle
One, Omi-COVID is not over.

30:12.296 --> 30:16.976 align:middle
And COVID can continue to
evolve, and it's just-we have

30:16.976 --> 30:18.866 align:middle
to accept that it's
not over, and the risks

30:18.866 --> 30:21.186 align:middle
to it can slow down growth.

30:21.886 --> 30:24.916 align:middle
And that would be-that's
sort of a downside risk

30:24.916 --> 30:26.056 align:middle
from a growth standpoint.

30:26.426 --> 30:28.806 align:middle
I would point to-you
know, another,

30:28.806 --> 30:34.886 align:middle
another risk is just
further, further problems

30:34.886 --> 30:38.236 align:middle
in the supply chains, which
could slow down activity.

30:38.286 --> 30:41.946 align:middle
And you see the situation in
China as a situation there

30:41.946 --> 30:48.556 align:middle
where that's-their no-COVID
policy may cause more lockdowns,

30:48.556 --> 30:51.776 align:middle
is likely to, and that
may play into-may play

30:51.776 --> 30:54.206 align:middle
into more problems
in supply chains.

30:54.566 --> 30:56.046 align:middle
In addition, there's
what's going

30:56.046 --> 30:57.596 align:middle
on in Eastern Europe
and things like that.

30:57.596 --> 30:59.656 align:middle
So there's plenty
of risk out there.

31:00.276 --> 31:04.826 align:middle
And we, you know, we
can't-we can't forget

31:04.826 --> 31:06.206 align:middle
that there are risks
on both sides.

31:06.256 --> 31:07.396 align:middle
So that's, that's there.

31:07.896 --> 31:09.886 align:middle
That's what I would say.

31:10.256 --> 31:13.346 align:middle
I know you've been all over this
issue with, with my colleagues,

31:13.346 --> 31:15.656 align:middle
Craig, on the issue
of, of information.

31:16.156 --> 31:18.636 align:middle
We don't-we don't have that
information at the Board.

31:18.636 --> 31:21.256 align:middle
And, you know, I hand-I
asked the inspector general

31:21.256 --> 31:25.786 align:middle
to do an investigation, and
that is out of my hands.

31:26.086 --> 31:27.246 align:middle
I play no role in it.

31:27.246 --> 31:29.116 align:middle
I seek to play no role in it.

31:29.636 --> 31:33.116 align:middle
And I don't-I really-I
can't help you here today

31:33.166 --> 31:33.906 align:middle
on this issue.

31:34.526 --> 31:36.186 align:middle
And I'm sorry I can't.

31:36.186 --> 31:36.676 align:middle
CRAIG TORRES.

31:37.446 --> 31:38.496 align:middle
Okay, okay.

31:38.496 --> 31:38.956 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

31:39.046 --> 31:40.186 align:middle
Thank you.

31:40.186 --> 31:41.206 align:middle
We'll go to Jeanna now.

31:41.206 --> 31:45.166 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

31:45.166 --> 31:46.586 align:middle
Thanks for taking our
questions, Chair Powell.

31:46.586 --> 31:47.756 align:middle
And sorry for my tech issues.

31:47.756 --> 31:49.896 align:middle
I wonder if you could
tell us a little bit

31:49.896 --> 31:53.096 align:middle
about where your thinking
on inflation stands today.

31:53.096 --> 31:56.636 align:middle
You know, the last time we
saw an SEP back in September,

31:56.966 --> 31:59.556 align:middle
we saw that you and your
colleagues were projecting

31:59.556 --> 32:02.006 align:middle
that inflation would sort of
sink back down quite close

32:02.006 --> 32:03.316 align:middle
to target by the
end of the year.

32:03.316 --> 32:05.726 align:middle
And I wonder if you still
think that projection

32:05.726 --> 32:07.606 align:middle
from December is
a reasonable one.

32:07.606 --> 32:09.336 align:middle
And if your thinking
has changed at all,

32:09.336 --> 32:11.026 align:middle
I wonder how you're
thinking about that.

32:11.026 --> 32:12.706 align:middle
And I also wonder if you
could talk a little bit

32:12.706 --> 32:15.226 align:middle
about the pathway to
getting to that deceleration.

32:15.226 --> 32:18.266 align:middle
Like, how do-how do we get
from here, 7 percent CPI,

32:18.266 --> 32:20.306 align:middle
to where you expect to be
at the end of the year?

32:20.756 --> 32:21.676 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

32:21.796 --> 32:26.846 align:middle
So I'd say, you know, since,
since the December meeting,

32:26.846 --> 32:32.656 align:middle
I would say that the inflation
situation is about the same

32:32.656 --> 32:34.266 align:middle
but probably slightly worse.

32:35.016 --> 32:38.356 align:middle
I'd be inclined to
raise my own estimate

32:38.356 --> 32:42.626 align:middle
of 2022 core PCE
inflation-let's just go

32:42.626 --> 32:45.346 align:middle
with that-by a few tenths today.

32:45.996 --> 32:48.476 align:middle
But we're not writing, writing
down an SEP at this meeting.

32:48.476 --> 32:50.226 align:middle
But I think it's-it
hasn't gotten better.

32:50.226 --> 32:52.156 align:middle
It's probably gotten
just a-just a bit worse,

32:52.396 --> 32:53.446 align:middle
and that's been the pattern.

32:53.956 --> 32:54.726 align:middle
That's been the pattern.

32:55.396 --> 32:57.966 align:middle
So I think if you look

32:57.966 --> 33:02.086 align:middle
at the-the FOMC participants
are-there's a range there.

33:02.876 --> 33:04.126 align:middle
And that range has been moving

33:04.126 --> 33:06.796 align:middle
to the right for,
for a year now.

33:07.106 --> 33:09.346 align:middle
And, by the way, if you
look at other forecasters,

33:09.426 --> 33:12.616 align:middle
essentially all other macro
forecasters who do this

33:12.616 --> 33:14.986 align:middle
for a living, you've
seen the same pattern.

33:15.526 --> 33:17.416 align:middle
So, what do we think about that?

33:17.716 --> 33:22.646 align:middle
Well, I think we, you know,
we wrote down rate increases

33:22.926 --> 33:25.496 align:middle
in the December meetings,
each of us individually.

33:26.096 --> 33:29.246 align:middle
And I think to the extent the
situation deteriorates further,

33:29.246 --> 33:30.896 align:middle
our policy will have
to address that,

33:31.666 --> 33:33.666 align:middle
if it deteriorates
meaningfully further,

33:33.666 --> 33:36.016 align:middle
either in the time
dimension or in the size

33:36.016 --> 33:37.196 align:middle
of the inflation dimension.

33:37.906 --> 33:39.456 align:middle
So that's how we're
thinking about it.

33:39.916 --> 33:43.566 align:middle
I-as I mentioned, though, I
think it's-part of this will be

33:43.566 --> 33:45.866 align:middle
that us, the Fed, moving away

33:45.866 --> 33:49.026 align:middle
from a very highly
accommodative policy

33:49.026 --> 33:51.696 align:middle
to a substantially less
accommodative policy and then,

33:51.756 --> 33:55.086 align:middle
over time, to a policy that's
not accommodative, in time.

33:55.086 --> 33:56.066 align:middle
I don't know when that will be.

33:56.066 --> 33:58.186 align:middle
That's-those are the things that
we're-that we're thinking about.

33:58.636 --> 34:00.526 align:middle
That's, that's part of it.

34:00.526 --> 34:01.586 align:middle
Another part of it is

34:01.806 --> 34:05.536 align:middle
that fiscal policy provided
an impulse to growth

34:05.536 --> 34:06.486 align:middle
over the last two years.

34:06.486 --> 34:07.996 align:middle
That impulse will
be less, in fact,

34:07.996 --> 34:09.636 align:middle
will be-will be negative
this year.

34:10.176 --> 34:11.106 align:middle
And so that's another thing.

34:11.106 --> 34:14.326 align:middle
The other one is, we will
eventually get relief

34:14.326 --> 34:15.226 align:middle
on the supply side.

34:15.476 --> 34:18.436 align:middle
And, you know, the ports
will be cleared up.

34:18.436 --> 34:21.436 align:middle
And there will be semiconductors
and things like that.

34:21.686 --> 34:25.316 align:middle
Now, what we're learning is,
it's just taking much longer,

34:25.316 --> 34:27.726 align:middle
so I think-longer than expected.

34:27.726 --> 34:31.096 align:middle
And that, I think, does
raise, raise the risk

34:31.246 --> 34:33.856 align:middle
that high inflation
will be more persistent.

34:34.766 --> 34:38.016 align:middle
I do think we'll come off
of the highs that we saw

34:38.016 --> 34:40.006 align:middle
in the early part
of this episode

34:40.006 --> 34:41.336 align:middle
in the-in the spring last year.

34:41.336 --> 34:43.766 align:middle
But, really, what's-the
question is going to be,

34:43.766 --> 34:45.186 align:middle
what is inflation running at?

34:45.906 --> 34:47.416 align:middle
And so we'll be watching that.

34:47.416 --> 34:49.856 align:middle
And I, you know,
we-our objective is

34:49.856 --> 34:51.806 align:middle
to get inflation back
down to 2 percent.

34:52.016 --> 34:54.246 align:middle
It's also to provide
enough support

34:54.246 --> 34:55.586 align:middle
to keep the labor
market healthy.

34:55.916 --> 34:58.816 align:middle
The labor market is very,
very strong right now,

34:59.376 --> 35:02.126 align:middle
and I think that that
strength will continue.

35:02.186 --> 35:04.486 align:middle
There's a-there's really
a shortage of workers.

35:04.486 --> 35:06.996 align:middle
We see it particularly
among production

35:06.996 --> 35:10.376 align:middle
and nonsupervisory workers and
people in the lowest quartile.

35:10.376 --> 35:12.526 align:middle
You see very large
wage increases.

35:13.186 --> 35:14.896 align:middle
I mentioned some of
the other indicators.

35:14.896 --> 35:17.686 align:middle
So I think that's, that's
what we're looking at.

35:17.686 --> 35:20.246 align:middle
And we're also-you know,
we realize, I think,

35:20.246 --> 35:23.706 align:middle
as everyone does, that this
outlook is quite uncertain

35:23.706 --> 35:26.366 align:middle
and that we're going
to have to adapt.

35:26.986 --> 35:29.676 align:middle
And we're going to communicate
as clearly as we can.

35:30.276 --> 35:33.756 align:middle
But we're going to have to
be adaptable and, you know,

35:33.756 --> 35:35.336 align:middle
move, move as appropriate.

35:35.336 --> 35:36.786 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

35:36.786 --> 35:37.396 align:middle
Great. Thank you.

35:37.396 --> 35:37.866 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

35:39.516 --> 35:40.206 align:middle
Thank you.

35:40.206 --> 35:42.876 align:middle
Let's go to Colby Smith.

35:44.656 --> 35:45.336 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

35:45.336 --> 35:46.316 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle.

35:46.536 --> 35:49.756 align:middle
Chair Powell, when you talk
about being humble and nimble

35:49.756 --> 35:52.036 align:middle
about the path forward
for monetary policy,

35:52.366 --> 35:54.146 align:middle
does that also include
the possibility

35:54.146 --> 35:57.016 align:middle
of raising interest rates by
larger increments and, say,

35:57.016 --> 35:59.696 align:middle
doing a 50 basis point
hike at some point

35:59.766 --> 36:02.206 align:middle
if inflation does not
moderate sufficiently?

36:02.476 --> 36:05.946 align:middle
And should we interpret
this approach as a departure

36:05.946 --> 36:08.556 align:middle
from the gradual pace that
we saw during the last

36:08.556 --> 36:10.586 align:middle
hiking cycle?

36:10.846 --> 36:11.296 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

36:11.586 --> 36:13.836 align:middle
So, as I mentioned, we have
not made these decisions.

36:13.836 --> 36:14.536 align:middle
We really haven't.

36:14.786 --> 36:17.096 align:middle
And, and what I can
tell you now, though,

36:17.096 --> 36:21.616 align:middle
is that we fully appreciate that
this is a different situation.

36:22.276 --> 36:25.956 align:middle
If you look back to where
we were in 2015, '16, '17,

36:25.956 --> 36:29.436 align:middle
'18 when we were raising
rates, inflation was very close

36:29.436 --> 36:31.236 align:middle
to 2 percent, even
below 2 percent.

36:31.276 --> 36:35.806 align:middle
Unemployment was, was not at our
estimates of the natural rate.

36:35.916 --> 36:39.916 align:middle
And growth was, you know,
in the 2 to 3 percent range.

36:40.616 --> 36:43.136 align:middle
Right now, we have inflation
running substantially

36:43.136 --> 36:45.146 align:middle
above 2 percent and,
and, you know,

36:45.146 --> 36:46.686 align:middle
more persistently
than we would like.

36:47.046 --> 36:49.106 align:middle
We have growth-even
in forecasts,

36:49.106 --> 36:50.816 align:middle
even in the somewhat
reduced forecast

36:50.816 --> 36:54.706 align:middle
for 2022-we still see
growth higher than,

36:54.706 --> 36:56.536 align:middle
substantially higher
than what we estimate

36:56.536 --> 36:58.866 align:middle
to be the potential growth rate.

36:58.866 --> 37:04.216 align:middle
And we see a labor market
where, by so many measures,

37:04.326 --> 37:05.776 align:middle
it is historically tight.

37:06.396 --> 37:10.066 align:middle
I think the-you know, in a way,
the least tight aspect of it is,

37:10.146 --> 37:11.926 align:middle
is looking at the
unemployment rate,

37:11.986 --> 37:14.606 align:middle
which is still below
our median estimate of,

37:14.726 --> 37:14.966 align:middle
of [the unemployment
rate consistent with]

37:15.496 --> 37:16.166 align:middle
maximum employment.

37:16.166 --> 37:18.976 align:middle
If you look at things like
quits and job openings,

37:18.976 --> 37:21.336 align:middle
as I mentioned earlier, and
wages, you're seeing-and,

37:21.336 --> 37:24.126 align:middle
and just the ratio
of job openings

37:24.126 --> 37:28.406 align:middle
to unemployed-you're seeing a
very, very tight labor market.

37:28.406 --> 37:32.976 align:middle
Now, we also know that
labor force participation is

37:33.106 --> 37:34.046 align:middle
significantly lower.

37:34.046 --> 37:38.026 align:middle
It's 1½ percentage points lower
than it was in February of 2020.

37:38.776 --> 37:41.636 align:middle
Maybe a [full] percentage point
of that is, is retirements.

37:41.736 --> 37:45.216 align:middle
Some part of those
retirements are, are, you know,

37:45.216 --> 37:47.666 align:middle
related to COVID rather than
just regular retirements.

37:47.666 --> 37:49.946 align:middle
So we think there's, there's
a pool of people out there

37:50.206 --> 37:51.886 align:middle
who could come back
into the labor force.

37:52.516 --> 37:54.586 align:middle
But it's not happening
very quickly,

37:54.586 --> 37:57.896 align:middle
and it may not-it may continue
to not happen very quickly

37:58.366 --> 38:01.196 align:middle
as long as the pandemic
is on[going].

38:01.296 --> 38:04.866 align:middle
So that's, that's, that's
how we think about that.

38:04.866 --> 38:07.756 align:middle
We haven't made-to your specific
question-we really have not

38:07.756 --> 38:08.936 align:middle
addressed those questions.

38:09.526 --> 38:11.996 align:middle
And we'll begin to address
them as we-as we move

38:11.996 --> 38:13.726 align:middle
into the March meeting
and meetings after that.

38:13.886 --> 38:14.206 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

38:20.186 --> 38:20.616 align:middle
Let's go to Rachel.

38:20.616 --> 38:21.146 align:middle
RACHEL SIEGEL.

38:25.136 --> 38:25.756 align:middle
Thank you, Michelle.

38:25.756 --> 38:27.716 align:middle
And thank you, Chair Powell,
for taking our questions.

38:27.716 --> 38:31.516 align:middle
I'm wondering if you can
talk to us about any metrics

38:31.516 --> 38:34.826 align:middle
that the Fed uses to assess
how inflation affects different

38:34.826 --> 38:38.456 align:middle
groups of Americans, especially
lower-, lower-income earners?

38:38.806 --> 38:41.106 align:middle
And are you worried that
the Fed underestimates,

38:41.106 --> 38:44.596 align:middle
or can't effectively measure,
the impact of inflation on some

38:44.596 --> 38:45.806 align:middle
of the most vulnerable
households?

38:45.806 --> 38:46.276 align:middle
Thank you.

38:46.276 --> 38:46.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

38:46.556 --> 38:51.896 align:middle
So it's, it's more a matter
of-I think the problem

38:52.416 --> 38:54.936 align:middle
that we're-that we're talking
about here is really that people

38:54.936 --> 38:58.196 align:middle
who are on fixed incomes who
are living paycheck to paycheck,

38:59.316 --> 39:03.166 align:middle
they're spending most
or all of their-of,

39:03.366 --> 39:07.756 align:middle
of what they're earning
on food, gasoline, rent,

39:08.196 --> 39:11.836 align:middle
heating their-heating, things
like that, basic necessities.

39:12.496 --> 39:17.776 align:middle
And so inflation right away,
right away forces people

39:17.776 --> 39:20.366 align:middle
like that to make very
difficult decisions.

39:20.956 --> 39:22.136 align:middle
So that's really the point.

39:22.136 --> 39:25.486 align:middle
I don't-I'm not aware
of, you know,

39:25.566 --> 39:28.016 align:middle
inflation literally
falling more on,

39:28.136 --> 39:30.726 align:middle
on different socioeconomic
groups.

39:31.016 --> 39:31.866 align:middle
It's-that's not the point.

39:31.866 --> 39:33.916 align:middle
The point is, some
people are just really

39:34.896 --> 39:37.826 align:middle
in a-prone to suffer more.

39:37.826 --> 39:40.566 align:middle
I mean, for people who
are economically well off,

39:41.236 --> 39:42.216 align:middle
inflation isn't good.

39:42.216 --> 39:46.156 align:middle
It's bad. High inflation is,
is bad, but they're going

39:46.156 --> 39:48.936 align:middle
to be able to continue to
eat and keep their homes

39:49.056 --> 39:51.046 align:middle
and drive their cars
and things like that.

39:51.046 --> 39:53.896 align:middle
It's more-so that, that's
really how I think of it.

39:56.086 --> 39:59.056 align:middle
And, you know, we, we
have to control inflation

39:59.056 --> 40:00.526 align:middle
for the benefit of
all Americans.

40:01.196 --> 40:05.146 align:middle
But part of-part of it is just
that it's particularly hard

40:05.146 --> 40:08.256 align:middle
on people with fixed incomes
and low incomes who spent most

40:08.256 --> 40:12.386 align:middle
of their-of their
income on necessities,

40:13.046 --> 40:15.556 align:middle
which are-which are
experiencing high inflation now.

40:15.816 --> 40:16.206 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:19.686 --> 40:20.036 align:middle
Thank you.

40:20.036 --> 40:21.026 align:middle
Let's go to Edward Lawrence.

40:21.026 --> 40:21.406 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

40:24.026 --> 40:25.606 align:middle
Thanks, Michelle, and
thank you, Mr. Chairman,

40:25.606 --> 40:26.776 align:middle
for taking the question here.

40:26.776 --> 40:29.566 align:middle
So year over year,
inflation's at a 40-year high.

40:29.566 --> 40:32.496 align:middle
The [increase in] input costs
for producer price index for all

40:32.496 --> 40:34.276 align:middle
of 2021 was the highest
on record.

40:34.276 --> 40:38.086 align:middle
Some investors fear that the
Fed might be moving too late.

40:38.086 --> 40:40.686 align:middle
Now, you said no decisions were
made on the path of rate hikes,

40:40.686 --> 40:45.246 align:middle
but was a rate hike more than
25 basis points discussed today?

40:45.246 --> 40:46.736 align:middle
And as a follow to
that-because I have problems

40:46.736 --> 40:50.226 align:middle
with the mute button-as a
follow to that, you testified

40:50.406 --> 40:52.126 align:middle
that the supply chain
issues could be worked

40:52.126 --> 40:52.756 align:middle
out by the end of the year.

40:52.756 --> 40:54.366 align:middle
You talked about that today.

40:54.366 --> 40:56.746 align:middle
The CEO of Ford, though,
told Fox Business today

40:56.746 --> 40:59.746 align:middle
that the chip shortage
will last into 2023.

40:59.746 --> 41:02.136 align:middle
So today you said inflation
will start to ease this year.

41:02.136 --> 41:05.526 align:middle
I want to drill down and get
a timeline that you see as to

41:05.526 --> 41:06.926 align:middle
when we could see that relief.

41:06.926 --> 41:07.216 align:middle
Thank you.

41:08.096 --> 41:08.506 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

41:08.576 --> 41:09.836 align:middle
So I, I would not say

41:10.176 --> 41:12.466 align:middle
that I would expect
the supply chain issues

41:12.466 --> 41:14.446 align:middle
to be completely worked out
by the end of this year.

41:14.446 --> 41:16.506 align:middle
I do not expect them, and
I have not expected them.

41:17.046 --> 41:18.766 align:middle
What I would say, and
I have been saying,

41:18.766 --> 41:22.226 align:middle
is that I expect progress to
be made in the second half

41:22.226 --> 41:24.406 align:middle
of this year, mainly-progress

41:24.406 --> 41:26.226 align:middle
because we're not
making much progress.

41:26.226 --> 41:29.916 align:middle
If you look at a ton of metrics,
you can find some that suggest

41:30.376 --> 41:33.306 align:middle
that delivery times are
shorter and inventories

41:33.306 --> 41:34.426 align:middle
in some industries moving up.

41:34.456 --> 41:37.846 align:middle
But, overall, we're
not-we're not making progress.

41:37.946 --> 41:40.076 align:middle
And, you know, things like the
semiconductor issue are going

41:40.076 --> 41:42.196 align:middle
to-they're going to
be quite a long time.

41:42.196 --> 41:45.806 align:middle
I would think they'll go
more than through 2023.

41:46.666 --> 41:52.006 align:middle
In terms of-so in terms of being
too late, I would just say this.

41:52.006 --> 41:54.266 align:middle
We-our policy needs
to be positioned

41:54.876 --> 41:57.256 align:middle
to address the full range of
plausible outcomes, as I said,

41:57.806 --> 42:00.876 align:middle
and particularly the possibility
that inflation will continue

42:00.876 --> 42:03.476 align:middle
to run higher, more
persistently than we'd expected.

42:04.416 --> 42:07.266 align:middle
And we think we are
positioned to make the changes

42:07.266 --> 42:08.756 align:middle
in our policy to do that, and,

42:08.756 --> 42:10.036 align:middle
and we're committed
to doing that.

42:10.426 --> 42:12.166 align:middle
And that's, that's
really where we are.

42:12.796 --> 42:15.636 align:middle
In terms of your, your
question about the,

42:15.706 --> 42:17.136 align:middle
the size of rate increases,

42:17.136 --> 42:19.256 align:middle
we haven't-we haven't
faced those decisions.

42:19.256 --> 42:20.156 align:middle
We haven't made them.

42:20.836 --> 42:23.836 align:middle
It isn't possible to sit
down here today and tell you

42:23.836 --> 42:26.266 align:middle
with any confidence what
the-what the precise path

42:26.946 --> 42:27.496 align:middle
will be.

42:28.076 --> 42:31.516 align:middle
But in-as we work
our way through this,

42:31.516 --> 42:34.736 align:middle
meeting by meeting, we are aware
that this is a very different,

42:35.376 --> 42:38.546 align:middle
different expansion, as
I've said a couple times,

42:38.546 --> 42:42.396 align:middle
with higher inflation, higher
growth, a much stronger economy.

42:43.066 --> 42:46.276 align:middle
And I think those
differences are likely

42:46.276 --> 42:50.386 align:middle
to be reflected in-in the
policy that we implement.

42:50.526 --> 42:50.986 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

42:53.656 --> 42:53.986 align:middle
Thank you.

42:53.986 --> 42:54.646 align:middle
Let's go to Mike McKee.

42:54.646 --> 42:55.026 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

42:59.486 --> 43:00.776 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

43:01.026 --> 43:03.206 align:middle
I'd like to sort of
weave some of the strands

43:03.206 --> 43:05.996 align:middle
of your answers together
and ask you, as you start

43:05.996 --> 43:08.666 align:middle
to reverse policy,
what your goal is.

43:08.846 --> 43:11.036 align:middle
Are you going to be
raising interest rates

43:11.166 --> 43:13.646 align:middle
until you get inflation
to 2 percent?

43:14.106 --> 43:17.586 align:middle
Do you want to go below 2
percent so that, on average,

43:17.586 --> 43:19.736 align:middle
you get a 2 percent
inflation rate?

43:19.736 --> 43:23.916 align:middle
And because you said we have
to protect the employment part

43:23.916 --> 43:26.306 align:middle
of your mandate, is there
some sort of circuit breaker

43:26.306 --> 43:29.436 align:middle
that would stop you from
raising interest rates

43:29.436 --> 43:30.366 align:middle
on the employment side?

43:30.366 --> 43:31.916 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

43:31.966 --> 43:34.326 align:middle
So, no. There's no-there's
nothing in our framework

43:34.326 --> 43:36.946 align:middle
about having inflation
run below 2 percent

43:37.586 --> 43:40.836 align:middle
so that we would do that,
try to achieve that outcome.

43:40.836 --> 43:42.596 align:middle
So the answer to
that is, is "no."

43:42.596 --> 43:45.456 align:middle
What we're trying to
do is get inflation,

43:46.246 --> 43:49.796 align:middle
keep inflation expectations
well anchored at 2 percent.

43:49.796 --> 43:52.586 align:middle
That's, that's always
the, the ultimate goal.

43:53.236 --> 43:58.276 align:middle
And we do that in the service
of having inflation-we get

43:58.276 --> 44:01.476 align:middle
to that goal by having inflation
average 2 percent over time.

44:01.476 --> 44:03.586 align:middle
And if inflation doesn't
average 2 percent over time,

44:03.586 --> 44:06.136 align:middle
then it's not clear why
inflation expectations would be

44:06.136 --> 44:07.356 align:middle
anchored at 2 percent.

44:07.776 --> 44:11.116 align:middle
So that, that's the way
we think about that.

44:13.746 --> 44:17.186 align:middle
You know, it-what was the last
part of your-of your question?

44:17.726 --> 44:18.676 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

44:18.676 --> 44:21.696 align:middle
I was asking if you're
protecting the employment side

44:21.696 --> 44:23.676 align:middle
of the mandate, whether
there's some sort

44:23.676 --> 44:24.746 align:middle
of circuit breaker there.

44:25.186 --> 44:25.946 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

44:25.946 --> 44:26.986 align:middle
Nothing like that.

44:26.986 --> 44:30.116 align:middle
I mean, I would say you
have a tremendously strong

44:30.426 --> 44:31.356 align:middle
labor market.

44:31.576 --> 44:34.846 align:middle
And you have growth
this year at-forecast

44:34.846 --> 44:40.456 align:middle
to be well above-well
above potential.

44:40.576 --> 44:42.676 align:middle
I mean, people who are
forecasting growth think

44:42.676 --> 44:43.996 align:middle
potential growth is
around 2 [percent].

44:44.226 --> 44:46.136 align:middle
Most forecasts [of actual
growth] are significantly

44:46.136 --> 44:49.276 align:middle
above that for 2022,
and that's even with,

44:49.406 --> 44:52.966 align:middle
with policy becoming
substantially

44:52.966 --> 44:53.726 align:middle
less accommodative.

44:53.726 --> 44:56.446 align:middle
So the labor market's going
to be strong for some time.

44:57.096 --> 44:58.796 align:middle
We're-ideally, what we're trying

44:58.796 --> 45:02.096 align:middle
to achieve is inflation
getting back down to 2 percent.

45:03.346 --> 45:05.866 align:middle
And we're trying to do that
in a process that, that,

45:06.146 --> 45:09.866 align:middle
that accomplishes-you know, that
will also leave the labor market

45:09.866 --> 45:10.936 align:middle
in a very strong position.

45:11.436 --> 45:13.916 align:middle
And no one really knows
what that will take.

45:14.306 --> 45:17.526 align:middle
Again, I would say that it isn't
just monetary policy that's

45:17.526 --> 45:18.656 align:middle
helping inflation get down.

45:18.656 --> 45:22.646 align:middle
It'll be supply-side
improvements, and it'll be less,

45:22.716 --> 45:23.426 align:middle
less fiscal, you know,

45:23.426 --> 45:25.786 align:middle
less fiscal impulse
in all likelihood.

45:26.326 --> 45:28.566 align:middle
So-but monetary policy
will do our job.

45:28.566 --> 45:30.576 align:middle
It is our job to get
inflation down to 2 percent.

45:31.036 --> 45:34.776 align:middle
And a situation where, where the
two goals are-the two goals can

45:34.776 --> 45:37.506 align:middle
be in tension is
a difficult one.

45:37.536 --> 45:39.816 align:middle
But I-but I don't really
think they are here, though,

45:39.816 --> 45:42.956 align:middle
because I think a
really significant threat

45:43.616 --> 45:46.146 align:middle
to further strengthening in
the labor market in the form

45:46.146 --> 45:49.716 align:middle
of higher participation
over time is high inflation.

45:50.346 --> 45:53.716 align:middle
And also, high inflation is
taking away the benefits of some

45:53.716 --> 45:57.906 align:middle
of these larger wage increases
that we're seeing now.

45:57.906 --> 46:00.316 align:middle
So we do hope to
achieve, and our plan is

46:00.316 --> 46:01.966 align:middle
to achieve, both of those goals.

46:02.976 --> 46:03.306 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

46:03.306 --> 46:05.786 align:middle
If I could-if I could
follow up, does the danger

46:05.786 --> 46:10.126 align:middle
of tightening too much as policy
works its way into the economy

46:10.126 --> 46:12.656 align:middle
with a lag mean that
you should go back

46:12.656 --> 46:15.996 align:middle
to being more forecast
dependent in making decisions,

46:15.996 --> 46:19.086 align:middle
rather than the state dependency
you've been using as a framework

46:19.086 --> 46:20.396 align:middle
for the last year
and a half or so?

46:20.396 --> 46:20.626 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

46:20.626 --> 46:25.136 align:middle
State dependency was
particularly around the thought

46:25.136 --> 46:30.136 align:middle
that if we-if we saw a
very strong labor market,

46:31.196 --> 46:34.746 align:middle
we would wait to see actual
inflation-actual inflation

46:35.326 --> 46:36.046 align:middle
before we tightened.

46:36.376 --> 46:38.736 align:middle
And so that was a very
state-dependent thought because,

46:39.176 --> 46:42.826 align:middle
for a long time, we'd been
tightening on the expectation

46:42.826 --> 46:44.356 align:middle
of high inflation,
which never appeared.

46:44.886 --> 46:47.556 align:middle
And that was the case for,
for a number of years.

46:48.056 --> 46:50.396 align:middle
So in this particular situation,

46:50.396 --> 46:54.246 align:middle
we will be clearly monitoring
incoming data as well

46:54.246 --> 46:55.316 align:middle
as the evolving outlook.

46:55.316 --> 46:55.636 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

47:05.466 --> 47:05.966 align:middle
Let's go to Michael Derby.

47:05.966 --> 47:06.446 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

47:06.446 --> 47:09.286 align:middle
Thank you for taking
my question.

47:09.606 --> 47:11.856 align:middle
I want to ask you, with
the benefit of hindsight,

47:11.856 --> 47:14.606 align:middle
and I realize, I mean,
that is what it is.

47:14.776 --> 47:18.266 align:middle
But do you feel that,
you know, monetary policy

47:18.266 --> 47:22.436 align:middle
and fiscal policy maybe did
too much to react to the crisis

47:22.436 --> 47:24.116 align:middle
and that part of the
inflation problem

47:24.116 --> 47:27.046 align:middle
that we're having right now is
because the government response,

47:27.046 --> 47:28.796 align:middle
you know, collectively was more

47:28.796 --> 47:30.566 align:middle
than what the economy
ended up needing?

47:32.196 --> 47:32.526 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

47:32.526 --> 47:35.736 align:middle
So I think it's too soon to
write that history, really.

47:35.736 --> 47:37.876 align:middle
But what I would say is this.

47:38.576 --> 47:43.556 align:middle
The-if you remember what it
felt like at the beginning

47:43.556 --> 47:46.986 align:middle
of the pandemic, literally
the global economy shutting

47:46.986 --> 47:49.806 align:middle
down in large part,
including our own economy,

47:49.806 --> 47:52.696 align:middle
and people going to their homes
for weeks on end in masks,

47:52.696 --> 47:56.686 align:middle
and there are no vaccines, and
it could be a really long time

47:56.686 --> 47:58.416 align:middle
to get them, you know.

47:58.846 --> 48:02.156 align:middle
And then, you know, you
have-economic activity drops

48:02.156 --> 48:05.136 align:middle
by a shocking amount in
one-so there was a real risk

48:05.136 --> 48:07.326 align:middle
of lasting damage.

48:08.006 --> 48:11.206 align:middle
And I think Congress
responded remarkably

48:11.206 --> 48:13.196 align:middle
with the-with the CARES Act,

48:13.196 --> 48:15.426 align:middle
incredibly timely
and very powerful.

48:16.056 --> 48:18.166 align:middle
People will-there'll always
be flaws in these things.

48:18.166 --> 48:21.896 align:middle
But in real time, it was
a remarkable achievement.

48:22.076 --> 48:23.406 align:middle
And we responded.

48:23.406 --> 48:26.806 align:middle
And what we were able to do was,
you know, stave off a collapse

48:26.806 --> 48:30.646 align:middle
of the financial system at
the beginning and make time

48:30.766 --> 48:32.026 align:middle
for what really needed
to happen,

48:32.026 --> 48:34.486 align:middle
which was the income replacement
and then the recovery

48:34.936 --> 48:36.656 align:middle
that Congress enabled
with the CARES Act.

48:36.656 --> 48:40.046 align:middle
So now, that was a
lot of-that was a lot.

48:40.666 --> 48:41.976 align:middle
And what we did was a lot.

48:42.556 --> 48:45.086 align:middle
And, you know, now-so,
what we have now is,

48:45.086 --> 48:48.486 align:middle
we have the strongest
recovery of any, any country.

48:48.486 --> 48:50.026 align:middle
And we have-we have a recovery

48:50.026 --> 48:52.906 align:middle
that looks completely unlike
other recoveries that we've had,

48:52.906 --> 48:56.216 align:middle
because we've, we've put so much
support behind the recovery.

48:57.026 --> 49:00.536 align:middle
And we're managing the, the
relatively high-class problems

49:00.536 --> 49:02.476 align:middle
that come with that,
which are high inflation

49:02.476 --> 49:03.366 align:middle
and a labor shortage.

49:04.166 --> 49:06.606 align:middle
So-and these are serious
problems, very serious problems

49:06.606 --> 49:10.876 align:middle
that we, you know, we're
working as hard as we can on.

49:11.226 --> 49:11.996 align:middle
Was it too much?

49:11.996 --> 49:14.046 align:middle
Again, I'm going to leave
that to the historians.

49:14.046 --> 49:18.176 align:middle
And-but look, in 25 years, we'll
look back at this incident,

49:18.666 --> 49:22.006 align:middle
which will be a, you know,
2-, 3-, 4-, 5-year period.

49:22.526 --> 49:23.926 align:middle
And we'll say, you know,

49:23.926 --> 49:26.326 align:middle
we'll have a-we'll have a much
better basis to make a judgment

49:26.326 --> 49:28.266 align:middle
about the actions
that people took.

49:28.966 --> 49:33.286 align:middle
But it was all founded, though,
in a-in a very strong reaction

49:33.286 --> 49:36.936 align:middle
to a, you know, to a
unique historical event.

49:37.216 --> 49:39.466 align:middle
And I guess I'll have
to leave it at that.

49:39.466 --> 49:41.976 align:middle
I look for-I hope I'll
be around to see how

49:41.976 --> 49:43.416 align:middle
that looks in 25 years.

49:43.566 --> 49:44.196 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

49:44.396 --> 49:46.216 align:middle
Fair enough.

49:46.216 --> 49:46.546 align:middle
Thank you.

49:46.546 --> 49:46.886 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

49:46.886 --> 49:47.156 align:middle
Thank you.

49:47.156 --> 49:51.526 align:middle
Let's go to Jean Yung.

49:51.526 --> 49:51.816 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

49:52.206 --> 49:52.776 align:middle
Thanks, Michelle.

49:52.776 --> 49:56.566 align:middle
Chair Powell, some investors are
expecting the yield curve could

49:56.566 --> 50:00.036 align:middle
flatten, or even invert,
after rate hikes begin.

50:00.346 --> 50:03.536 align:middle
Would that worry you, and
how important is that risk

50:03.536 --> 50:06.086 align:middle
in the Fed's consideration
for adjusting policy?

50:06.086 --> 50:07.556 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

50:07.696 --> 50:14.336 align:middle
So we, we do monitor the
slope of the yield curve,

50:14.336 --> 50:16.156 align:middle
but we don't control the
slope of the yield curve.

50:16.996 --> 50:20.656 align:middle
Many flat-many factors influence
longer-term interest rates.

50:21.136 --> 50:23.276 align:middle
But it is something that we
watch, and, and you will know

50:23.276 --> 50:26.236 align:middle
that from when we had this
issue a few years ago.

50:27.026 --> 50:28.716 align:middle
And we take it into account,

50:28.716 --> 50:31.146 align:middle
along with many other
financial conditions,

50:31.276 --> 50:33.646 align:middle
as we try to assess
the implications

50:33.646 --> 50:35.506 align:middle
of all those conditions
for the economic outlook.

50:35.766 --> 50:39.306 align:middle
So that's, that's one
thing I would say.

50:39.306 --> 50:43.196 align:middle
Another is, currently,
you've got a slope.

50:43.196 --> 50:45.966 align:middle
If you think about 2s
to 10s, 2-year Treasury

50:45.966 --> 50:48.626 align:middle
to 10-year Treasury, I think
that's around 75 basis points.

50:49.296 --> 50:50.536 align:middle
That's well within the range

50:50.536 --> 50:53.866 align:middle
of a normal-of a normal
yield-curve slope.

50:54.636 --> 50:58.616 align:middle
So it's something
we're monitoring.

50:58.616 --> 51:00.116 align:middle
We don't think of it
as-I don't think of it

51:00.116 --> 51:01.536 align:middle
as some kind of an iron law.

51:02.306 --> 51:05.546 align:middle
But we do look at it and try
to understand the implications

51:05.546 --> 51:06.626 align:middle
and what it's telling us.

51:06.626 --> 51:08.786 align:middle
And it's-but it's one of
many things that we monitor.

51:09.136 --> 51:09.366 align:middle
JEAN YUNG.

51:11.246 --> 51:15.606 align:middle
Can I follow up real quick and
ask, if it-if it did invert,

51:15.606 --> 51:18.486 align:middle
would you tie it to
U.S. fundamentals?

51:18.486 --> 51:23.326 align:middle
Or would it be driven by a
much broader set of factors?

51:23.326 --> 51:23.926 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

51:23.926 --> 51:26.156 align:middle
We'd-that's, that's a good
question in, in real time.

51:27.086 --> 51:30.166 align:middle
Obviously, U.S. long-term
sovereign debt is an-is an

51:30.166 --> 51:31.916 align:middle
important global asset.

51:32.616 --> 51:35.686 align:middle
And it-and the fact that our
rates are so much higher than,

51:35.686 --> 51:39.236 align:middle
than other risk-free
sovereign rates

51:39.236 --> 51:40.366 align:middle
around the world
may put something

51:40.366 --> 51:42.126 align:middle
of a ceiling on our-on
our rates.

51:42.126 --> 51:42.556 align:middle
I don't know.

51:43.036 --> 51:45.286 align:middle
But it would really
depend on the-on the facts

51:45.286 --> 51:46.616 align:middle
and circumstances at that time.

51:46.686 --> 51:47.246 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

51:49.646 --> 51:50.056 align:middle
Thank you.

51:50.056 --> 51:52.016 align:middle
Let's go to Brian Cheung
for the last question.

51:52.016 --> 51:52.526 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

51:54.086 --> 51:54.566 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Powell.

51:54.566 --> 51:55.906 align:middle
Brian Cheung, Yahoo Finance.

51:55.906 --> 51:59.346 align:middle
Within the context of just the
broad effort to normalize rates,

51:59.346 --> 52:02.736 align:middle
would you describe what you
want to do as a gradual hiking?

52:02.736 --> 52:05.876 align:middle
And then, secondly, within
the context of hiking cycles,

52:05.876 --> 52:08.816 align:middle
it's often the talking point for
financial stability and wanting

52:08.816 --> 52:11.606 align:middle
to make sure that asset
bubbles don't emerge.

52:11.606 --> 52:14.396 align:middle
Is that something that has also
factored into the conversation

52:14.636 --> 52:15.936 align:middle
as you start to think
about hiking rates?

52:15.936 --> 52:19.426 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR POWELL.

52:19.426 --> 52:23.086 align:middle
I would describe what we're
doing along these lines.

52:23.476 --> 52:27.616 align:middle
This is going to be a year in
which we move steadily away

52:27.976 --> 52:30.416 align:middle
from the very highly
accommodative monetary policy

52:30.416 --> 52:31.596 align:middle
that we put in place to deal

52:31.596 --> 52:33.836 align:middle
with the-with the economic
effects of the pandemic.

52:34.876 --> 52:36.506 align:middle
And that's going to
involve a number of things.

52:36.506 --> 52:39.606 align:middle
It's going to involve and
does involve finishing

52:39.606 --> 52:40.526 align:middle
asset purchases.

52:41.236 --> 52:42.906 align:middle
It's going to involve
lifting off.

52:44.276 --> 52:46.416 align:middle
And it's going to involve
additional rate increases

52:46.556 --> 52:47.406 align:middle
as appropriate.

52:47.406 --> 52:49.216 align:middle
And we, we have-we're
going to write

52:49.216 --> 52:50.776 align:middle
down in March our
next assessment

52:50.776 --> 52:51.656 align:middle
of what that might be.

52:52.496 --> 52:54.706 align:middle
It's going to continue to
evolve as the data evolve.

52:54.706 --> 52:56.806 align:middle
We need to be quite
adaptable, I think,

52:56.986 --> 52:58.646 align:middle
in our understanding of this.

52:59.456 --> 53:01.096 align:middle
The last thing we're
going to do is,

53:01.096 --> 53:03.676 align:middle
we're going to have a couple
more meetings, I think,

53:03.676 --> 53:07.496 align:middle
to talk about allowing
the balance sheet to begin

53:07.496 --> 53:11.736 align:middle
to run off and [to] do so
in a predictable manner.

53:11.806 --> 53:13.216 align:middle
And that's, that's something

53:13.216 --> 53:16.456 align:middle
that we will also be
doing as appropriate.

53:16.706 --> 53:19.966 align:middle
And I wouldn't-I don't
think it's possible

53:19.966 --> 53:24.246 align:middle
to say exactly how
this is going to go.

53:24.246 --> 53:26.656 align:middle
And we're, we're
going to need to be,

53:26.926 --> 53:29.426 align:middle
as I've mentioned,
nimble about this.

53:30.116 --> 53:32.856 align:middle
And the economy is quite
different this time.

53:33.026 --> 53:34.466 align:middle
I've said this several
times now.

53:34.516 --> 53:35.716 align:middle
The economy is quite different.

53:35.806 --> 53:36.496 align:middle
It's stronger.

53:36.496 --> 53:37.416 align:middle
Inflation is higher.

53:37.476 --> 53:40.156 align:middle
The labor market is much,
much stronger than it was.

53:40.566 --> 53:44.416 align:middle
And growth is above trend, even
this year, let alone last year.

53:44.416 --> 53:47.116 align:middle
So all of those things are
going to go into our thinking

53:47.116 --> 53:49.106 align:middle
as we make-as we
make monetary policy.

53:49.636 --> 53:53.936 align:middle
And you asked about
financial stability concerns.

53:54.816 --> 53:58.846 align:middle
In connection with our
policy-was that your question?

53:59.146 --> 53:59.736 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

54:00.956 --> 54:03.106 align:middle
Yes. Within the context
of other hiking cycles,

54:03.106 --> 54:06.016 align:middle
it seems like [a set of] worries
about asset bubbles emerging

54:06.016 --> 54:08.446 align:middle
as a result of easy rates
has been part of that .

54:08.446 --> 54:09.806 align:middle
I didn't know if that was
part of the discussion today.

54:10.706 --> 54:11.146 align:middle
CHAIR POWELL.

54:11.376 --> 54:12.376 align:middle
I would just say this.

54:13.076 --> 54:15.466 align:middle
We, we, of course, have a
financial stability framework.

54:16.016 --> 54:19.876 align:middle
And what it shows is a number
of, of positive aspects

54:19.876 --> 54:20.976 align:middle
of financial stability.

54:20.976 --> 54:22.566 align:middle
But you mentioned, really,

54:22.566 --> 54:24.326 align:middle
[the behavior of] asset
prices is one of the four.

54:24.836 --> 54:26.966 align:middle
So asset prices are
somewhat elevated,

54:26.966 --> 54:29.766 align:middle
and they reflect a high risk
appetite and that sort of thing.

54:30.356 --> 54:33.896 align:middle
I don't really think asset
prices themselves represent a

54:33.896 --> 54:36.136 align:middle
significant threat to
financial stability,

54:36.136 --> 54:38.106 align:middle
and that's because
households are

54:38.106 --> 54:40.296 align:middle
in good shape financially
than they have been.

54:40.646 --> 54:42.776 align:middle
Businesses are in good
shape financially.

54:42.806 --> 54:45.596 align:middle
Defaults on business loans are
low and that kind of thing.

54:45.596 --> 54:48.716 align:middle
The banks are highly
capitalized with high liquidity

54:48.716 --> 54:51.236 align:middle
and quite resilient and strong.

54:51.846 --> 54:53.746 align:middle
There are some concerns

54:53.746 --> 54:57.126 align:middle
in the-in the nonbank
financial sector around-still

54:57.126 --> 54:58.066 align:middle
around money market funds,

54:58.066 --> 55:01.906 align:middle
although the SEC is making-has
made some very positive

55:01.906 --> 55:02.636 align:middle
proposals there.

55:03.196 --> 55:04.826 align:middle
And we also saw some things

55:04.826 --> 55:07.856 align:middle
in the Treasury market during
the acute phase of the crisis,

55:07.856 --> 55:10.656 align:middle
which, which we're
looking at ways to address.

55:10.656 --> 55:14.786 align:middle
But, overall, the financial
stability vulnerabilities are

55:14.786 --> 55:16.706 align:middle
manageable-are manageable,
I would say.

55:16.856 --> 55:17.386 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

55:20.406 --> 55:20.936 align:middle
Thank you very much.

